             \subcaption[]{A level of Jelly-No}
            \label{jellyexample}
            \end{subfigure}
            \begin{subfigure}{0.45\textwidth}
                \centering
                 \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{Intro/JellyNo_fusion.png}
                 \subcaption[]{The merging mechanism in Jelly-No}
                 \label{jelfusion}
            \end{subfigure}
       \end{figure}

        Hanano (see Figure \ref{hanexample}) is a 2D side-view puzzle game with gravity represented by a grid of size $n\times{m}$, composed of coloured movable blocks of size $1\times 1$, coloured flowers which are represented by blocks of size $1\times 1$ marked with a flower pattern, movable grey blocks of any size or shape and fixed, non-movable platforms. The coloured blocks and flowers can be red, blue or yellow. Flowers are all attached to a block or platform and can only move if the block they are attached to is moved. 

        In this game, the player can either move a block to the right or left by one block, or horizontally swap two adjacent blocks of size $1 \times 1$. Coloured blocks are marked with an arrow: if a coloured block touches a flower of the same colour, and if there is enough space to allow it, this block blooms and a new flower appears attached to that block in the direction of the arrow. Note that this new flower can push movable blocks around it in order to make enough space to bloom (see Figure \ref{hanbloom} where flowers are marked with stripes and the letter "F", light grey rectangles represent fixed platforms and dark grey rectangles represent movable blocks), and if this new flower touches a movable block of similar colour, this second block can also bloom, creating a chain reaction. 

        All movable blocks are subject to gravity, which means that in the absence of support of a block, flower or platform they fall until they reach one. 

        The goal of the game is to make all the coloured blocks bloom. The detailed rules can be found in \cite{HananoJ}.

