\section{\textsc{Jelly} with black jellies and no bound on the size of the board}
\label{generalJelly}

    We study the complexity of \textsc{Jelly} with no bound on the size of the board. Our main result is that allowing black jellies in 1-Colour \textsc{Jelly} renders the game PSPACE-Complete.

    We prove first that it is PSPACE-Complete with an unbounded number of colours, then with the restriction that jellies can either be of the same colour or be black jellies.

    \subsection{Previous works}

    To prove PSPACE-Hardness in both cases, we construct a reduction from a variation of the Nondeterministic Constraint Logic (NCL) problem, a graph problem presented and studied by Robert A. Hearn and Erik D. Demaine\cite{ncl}. This problem was used by Michael C. Chavrimootoo to study \textsc{Hanano} in \cite{HananoJ} and is very useful to prove PSPACE-hardness of games of sliding or pushing blocks. 

    Our reduction borrows many ideas from the work of Michael C.\~ Chavrimootoo\cite{HananoJ}, notably the use of visibility representations and the idea of representing vertices as vertex gadgets to be solved and flows as blocks (in our case jellies) travelling between the vertex gadgets through horizontal tunnels. Note that our work mostly uses ideas from a preliminary version of this paper, dated from 2022 which is in Arxiv\cite{HananoARXIV}. The published versions from 2023 \cite{Hanano} and 2025 \cite{HananoJ} greatly reduce the number of gadgets to build in the case of \textsc{Hanano} (it is an open question whether we can reduce the number of gadgets used in the case of \textsc{Jelly}).\\

    A major difference between the two games is that in \textsc{Hanano}, the blooming mechanism allows to push a block upward by creating a new flower block. In \textsc{Jelly} (at least for the simplest version of the game that we consider in this work), there is no way for a jelly to go upward and no new block of jelly can be created. Besides, in \textsc{Jelly}, solving a level of the game requires to reunite all blocks of some similar type to one same place, which is not the case in \textsc{Hanano}. All these differences required to adapt the ideas presented in \cite{HananoJ} in order to build suitable gadgets.

    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

    \subsection{\textsc{Jelly} with an unbounded number of colours}

    The fact that \textsc{Jelly} is in PSPACE can be proven by using standard arguments. The proof can be found in Section \ref{sec:inPspace} (Theorem \ref{inPspace}). In this paragraph and the next one, we shall focus our efforts in proving that the game is in fact PSPACE-Hard.

    First, we study the complexity of \textsc{Jelly} with an unbounded number of different colours for the jellies. We prove the following result:

    \begin{theorem}
        \label{JellyPHard}
        \textsc{Jelly} with an unbounded number of colours is PSPACE-Hard.
    \end{theorem}

    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

        \subsubsection{NCL graph and visibility representation}

        An NCL graph G = (E, V) is a directed graph that meets the following constraints:
        \begin{enumerate}
            \item Edges are of weight either one (red edges) or two (blue edges).
            \item For every vertex, the incoming flow is at least two (we call this constraint the minimum inflow requirement).
        \end{enumerate}

        Given such graph G, given an edge $e \in E$, the problem is deciding whether there exists a sequence of edge direction flips such that the direction of $e$ can be flipped and the resulting graph is still NCL. Another way to view this problem, which is the one used in \cite{ncl}, is to consider the graph as undirected and each assignment of directions as a configuration: moving from one configuration to another means flipping the direction of an edge such that the resulting configuration still meets the incoming flow requirement constraint. The problem would then be to find a sequence of configurations that result in $e$ being flipped. This problem happens to be PSPACE-complete even with the following additional constraints\cite{ncl} (see Figure \ref{NCL}):

        \begin{enumerate}  
            \setcounter{enumi}{2}
            \item All vertices have exactly three incident edges.
            \item Vertices follow two possible patterns: AND (two incident red edges and one incident blue edge) or OR (all three incident edges are blue) (see Figure \ref{AndOr}).
            \item The graph is simple (without loops or multiple edges) and planar (it can be drawn in two dimensions without any of its edges intersecting).
        \end{enumerate}

        \begin{figure}[H]
             \begin{subfigure}{0.50\textwidth}
                \centering
                 \includegraphics[width=0.55\textwidth]{PSPACE/NCL.png}
                 \subcaption[]{Example of a graph respecting our constraints}
                 \label{NCL}
             \end{subfigure}
             \begin{subfigure}{0.50\textwidth}
                \centering
                 \includegraphics[width=1\textwidth]{PSPACE/AndOr.png}
                 \subcaption[]{OR and AND patterns}
                \label{AndOr}
             \end{subfigure}
        \end{figure}

        In order to reduce the NCL problem to \textsc{Jelly}, all we need to do is construct gadgets to emulate AND and OR vertices as well as a mechanism for flipping edges and ensure that all the constraints are respected. However, due to gravity, some moves in \textsc{Jelly} are irreversible, which is not the case in the NCL problem. The problem of circumventing the effects of gravity in \textsc{Hanano} was treated very efficiently in \cite{HananoJ} by using the notion of visibility representation: a graph representation for planar graphs studied notably by Roberto Tamassia and Ioannis G.Tollis, who presented definitions as well as linear-time algorithms for constructing such representations\cite{Tamassia1986}. The idea used in \cite{HananoJ}, which we will reuse here, was to work not from the graph G but from its visibility representation.

        Given a planar graph G = (E, V), a visibility representation is a 2D representation of G that maps vertices to vertical segments and edges to horizontal ones (possibly arrows if G is directed) such that if two vertices are linked by an edge then the corresponding vertical segments are also linked by a horizontal segment and apart from this situation, segments do not intersect each other.

        \begin{figure}[H]
            \centering
            \includegraphics[width=0.38\textwidth]{PSPACE/visibility_example.png}
            \caption{Visibility representation corresponding to graph \ref{NCL}, with vertices represented by rectangles instead of vertical segments for a better visibility.}
            \label{visibrep}
        \end{figure}

        For the sake of our reduction, we also assume that two horizontal segments do not share $y$ coordinates. We can note that in \cite{Tamassia1986} the vertices were represented by horizontal segments and the edges by vertical ones, but the definition used in our work, which is also the one used in \cite{HananoJ}, is equivalent and more adapted to the purpose of our reduction.

        Given the visibility representation for G, each vertex can be symbolised by the edges to which it is connected and their position relatively to the other incident edges: left or right and top, middle or bottom, using B or R for segments representing respectively blue or red edges and $\cdot$ to indicate that a position is not occupied by an edge. For example, in Figure \ref{visibrep}, vertex A can be represented by ( $\cdot,\cdot,\cdot$ | B, R, R), meaning it has no incident edge on its left side, a blue edge at the top of its right side and red edges at the middle and bottom of its right side; vertex B can be represented by ( $\cdot$, B, $\cdot$ | B, $\cdot$, B), vertex C by (B, $\cdot$, R | $\cdot$, R, $\cdot$) and vertex D by (B, R, R | $\cdot, \cdot, \cdot$ ). This notation, similar to the one used in \cite{HananoJ}, allows us to list all the possible configurations for vertices in a visibility representation. 

        With these tools and notations in mind, we can now build our reduction.

        %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

        \subsubsection{Reduction from NCL to \textsc{Jelly}}

        Let us first present a global idea of the reduction through an example, before detailing the construction.

        Starting from a given NCL graph (see Figure \ref{NCL}), we first compute the corresponding visibility representation:

        \begin{figure}[H]
            \centering
            \includegraphics[width=0.38\textwidth]{PSPACE/visibility_example_coloured.png}
            \caption{Visibility representation where each vertex is represented by a distinct colour}
            \label{coloured}
        \end{figure}

        Each vertex will be represented by an appropriate gadget, and each edge $e_i = (u_i, v_i) \in E$ by a horizontal tunnel linking the two vertex gadgets for $u_i$ and $v_i$.
        The flows will be represented by horizontal jellies travelling through these tunnels. If the jelly representing some edge $e$ is in vertex gadget $u$, it corresponds to $e$ being oriented toward $u$ in the graph.

        Let $e = (A, B)$ be the edge which we want to flip to $(B, A)$. This representation gives us the following level (Figure \ref{fullLevel}), where a fixed, extra jelly with the same colour as the one corresponding to $e$ is added at the bottom of the gadget corresponding to $A$ to enforce the condition that $e$ must be flipped. The jelly for $e$ and the required extra jelly are circled in blue and each vertex gadget has the colour corresponding to the one in Figure \ref{coloured}:

        \begin{figure}[H]
            \centering
            \includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{PSPACE/example_reduction.png}
            \caption{Corresponding reduction}
            \label{fullLevel}
        \end{figure}

        Let us now detail the proof of Theorem \ref{JellyPHard}.

        \begin{proof}

        We consider the following problem:

        \textsc{Instance:} Let $G = (E, V)$ be a simple planar NCL graph such that all vertices have exactly three incident edges and follow either an OR or AND pattern. Let $e = (u, v) \in E$. Is there a succession of edge flips such that $e$ can be flipped and the resulting graph is still NCL?

        To reduce our problem to \textsc{Jelly}, we begin by computing the visibility representation corresponding to G, which can be done in linear time\cite{Tamassia1986}. In the rest of the paragraph, we will name by "vertices" and "edges" the corresponding vertical shapes and horizontal segments in the representation.

        Since our version of \textsc{Jelly} has an unbounded number of colours, we can assign one colour to each vertex and one colour to each edge of our original graph. Let $n_i, b_i^1, b_i^2, b_i^3, i \in [1, |V|]$ and $m_j, j \in [1, |E|]$ be the different colours used in our reduction.

        Due to the structure used, instead of only having to build gadgets for AND and OR patterns, we now need to build vertex gadgets corresponding to each possible configuration of adjacent edges in the visibility representation. Keeping in mind the assumption that no edge shares a $y$ coordinate, i.e. there can only be one incident edge at each vertical position (top, middle, bottom), in an OR pattern since all incident edges are blue there are only two possibilities for each vertical position (the incident blue edge is on the right or left), i.e. $2^{3} = 8$ total configurations. In an AND pattern there are six possibilities for the incident blue edge and then four for the two red edges, i.e. 24 total configurations. Due to symmetry, the number of gadgets to build can be reduced from 32 to 16.

        Vertices will be represented by gadgets which we will call "vertex gadgets", according to the disposition of their adjacent edges in the visibility representation, as described in the rest of this paragraph. Those vertex gadgets are linked by tunnels of height 1, which correspond to the edges.

        In tunnels corresponding to blue edges, we place a jelly of width 2 and height 1, which we will call a "blue edge jelly" (or "blue jelly" for short), and similarly in tunnels corresponding to red edges, a "red (edge) jelly" of size 1$\times$1, such that each jelly corresponding to edge $e_j$ has a unique colour $m_j$. Another option, equivalent to using distinct colours, is to use black jellies which cannot merge. These edge jellies can move from one incident vertex gadget to the other through their assigned tunnel, representing a change of direction of the corresponding edge. 

        For readability purposes we represent blue (resp. red) edge jellies in blue (resp. red) on the figures, but with variations of patterns to indicate that in the reduction, they should all either have distinct colours or be black jellies. Our constructions also use these black jellies, which we will represent in dark grey for readability purposes. Light grey blocks will represent fixed, unmovable walls and platforms. Note that these black jellies could also be represented using unique colours $b_i^1, b_i^2, b_i^3$ for each and therefore we do not strictly need to use this feature.

        For each gadget, we must make sure that:
        \begin{enumerate}
            \item {Edge jellies can only access the two vertex gadgets incident to their assigned tunnel, and no other jelly can travel through this tunnel.}
            \item{At every step of the game, each gadget must contain at least one blue edge jelly or two red edge jellies; otherwise the puzzle becomes unsolvable.}
            \item{As long as the part of the puzzle corresponding to some vertex gadget is not solved and the previous constraint is respected, moving the edge jellies in and out of the gadget must be reversible.}
            \item{The height of the vertex gadgets, especially the vertical space between the tunnels, should easily be modified to fit the visibility representation, where vertices have different heights to make sure the tunnels do not cross.}
        \end{enumerate}

        All gadgets use a similar idea: we place the entries for all three tunnels at the corresponding position in the vertex gadget and the corresponding edge jelly in this tunnel. 

        At the bottom of the gadget, there are two jellies of the same colour $n_i$ (specific to each vertex) separated by a gap of width 2 and height 1, which we will call "vertex jellies". One of these jellies, on the right, is fixed inside the wall so it cannot move. The other, on the left, can move. The only way for the jellies to merge is for the gap to be filled by using either one blue edge jelly or two red edge jellies. Various black jellies can either help or block them while ensuring that the constraints are respected at any given time.

        Let us now consider the edge jelly corresponding to edge $e$ chosen at the beginning of the game, which we want to flip from $(u,v)$ to $(v,u)$. This corresponds in our reduction to forcing the edge jelly to be in the vertex gadget corresponding to $u$ at the end of the game. To ensure this constraint, we add a jelly of the same colour in the ground of the gap at the bottom of the vertex gadget corresponding to $u$. Therefore, the level can only be completed if the edge jelly is in this gadget. This corresponds to fixing the direction of the edge. \\

        We start by constructing the OR vertex gadgets, as visible in Figures \ref{Solved1} to \ref{Solved4}, which also present indications on how to solve each of those gadgets.

        In these constructions, the black jelly can always be supported by at least one of the blue edge jellies. However, if it were to fall without one of the blue jellies being at a right position to solve the puzzle (for example under it or between two of its "branches"), it would block access to either the gap or the fixed vertex jelly at the bottom and prevent the puzzle from being solved.

        \begin{figure}[H]
            \centering
             \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{PSPACE/OR_jelly/solved/_-BBB--.png}
             \caption[width=0.8\textwidth]{(B, $\cdot, \cdot$ | $\cdot$, B, B) OR gadget.}
             \label{Solved1}
        \end{figure}
        \begin{figure}[H]
            \centering
             \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{PSPACE/OR_jelly/solved/solvedB-B-B-.png}
             \caption[width=0.8\textwidth]{(B, $\cdot$, B | $\cdot$, B, $\cdot$) OR gadget.}
            \label{Solved2}
        \end{figure}
        \begin{figure}[H]
            \centering
             \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{PSPACE/OR_jelly/solved/BB---B.png}
             \caption[width=0.8\textwidth]{(B, B, $\cdot$ | $\cdot$, $\cdot$, B) OR gadget.}
            \label{Solved3}
         \end{figure}
        \begin{figure}[H]
            \centering
             \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{PSPACE/OR_jelly/solved/solvedBBB---.png}
             \caption[width=0.8\textwidth]{(B, B, B, | $\cdot$, $\cdot$, $\cdot$) OR gadget.}
            \label{Solved4}
        \end{figure}

        The next step in the reduction is constructing the AND vertex gadgets and ensuring that at any given time, at least one blue or two red jellies must be present in the gadget for the level to be solvable. These gadgets use the same idea as the previous ones but are usually slightly more complicated, as they require two or even three black jellies and more complex shapes to ensure that the constraint is respected. In these gadgets, the gap at the bottom can either be filled by the blue jelly or by the two red jellies.\\

        The AND configurations can be divided into three categories depending on the position of the blue edge: top, middle or bottom. 

        We start with the constructions where the blue edge is at the bottom, which use easier shapes for the black jellies: besides, the gadgets for configuration (R, $\cdot$, $\cdot$ | $\cdot$, R, B) can easily be adapted for the configuration ($\cdot$, R, $\cdot$ | R, $\cdot$, B) by switching the heights of the two black jellies, therefore we will only present three configurations instead of four.

        When the two red jellies are on the same side of the vertex gadget, solving it with those can be achieved by pushing the black jellies in order to form a "chimney" leading to the bottom of the gadget, as visible in Figures \ref{Solved5} to \ref{Solved7}.

        \begin{figure}[H]
            \centering
             \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{PSPACE/AND_bottom/solved/R---RB.png}
             \caption[width=0.9\textwidth]{(R, $\cdot, \cdot$ | $\cdot$, R, B) AND gadget.}
             \label{Solved5}
        \end{figure}
        \begin{figure}[H]
            \centering
             \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{PSPACE/AND_bottom/solved/RR---B.png}
             \caption[width=0.9\textwidth]{(R, R $\cdot$ | $\cdot$, $\cdot$, B) AND gadget.}
            \label{Solved6}
