        Besides, we can easily check that the other constraints are respected for all the gadgets by checking that both resolutions are possible (one blue jelly or two red jellies) and that if the constraints are not respected, the gadget cannot be solved, either because the tunnels allowing the edge jellies to enter the vertex gadget get blocked by the black jellies or because these jellies fall at the bottom of the gadget and prevent the two vertex jellies from merging. \\

         It is easy to see that if our instance of restricted NCL has a solution, i.e. if given an edge $e \in E$ there exists a succession of edge flips such that the direction of $e$ can be flipped and the resulting graph still meets the constraints, then the level of \textsc{Jelly} resulting from our reduction also has a solution: for each edge flip $(v_1, u_1)$ to $(u_1, v_1)$ in the solution, the corresponding edge jelly, initially in the vertex gadget corresponding to $u_1$, can be moved to the one corresponding to $v_1$. If at every step of this succession of flips the minimum inflow requirement is still met in the graph, then the construction of each gadget will stay in place for the duration of the game: eventually, each vertex gadget can be solved by filling the gap with either one blue or two red edge jellies and the edge jelly corresponding to $e$ can be merged with the corresponding jelly present in the vertex gadget for $u$. 

         Now, we must ensure that if the level of \textsc{Jelly} constructed by our reduction is solvable, then the corresponding instance of NCL has a solution.

         Due to the construction of the gadgets, the only way for the game to be solved is if (1) the gap in every gadget is filled by either one blue jelly or two red jellies and (2) the jelly corresponding to $e$ can be merged with the jelly in the gadget corresponding to $u$. 

         This corresponds exactly to
         (1) meeting the minimum inflow requirement constraint in every vertex gadget and at every step of the game (if there exists a move and a vertex gadget for which the requirement is violated then the construction in this gadget falls apart and the black jellies fall down, thus the edge jellies cannot reach the gap and allow vertex jellies to merge, making the level impossible to finish) and
         (2) flipping the direction of edge $e$, which was the goal to reach.

         Therefore the original problem has a solution which can be determined by following the succession of moves leading to a winning configuration of the level.

        This concludes our proof of Theorem \ref{JellyPHard}.
        \end{proof}

        Note that if we use black jellies for all the edges other than $e$, then we only need |V| + 1 colours for our reduction: one for each vertex of $G$ and one for $e$. If we forbid the use of black jellies, then since vertex gadgets use three extra jellies at most, we need to add at most $3 \cdot |V|$ colours for those and one colour for each edge so we need at most $4 \cdot |V| + |E|$ colours for the entire reduction.

        With some modifications in the gadgets, this result can actually be strengthened by reducing the number of colours for jellies other than the black jellies to only one. 

    \subsection{\textsc{Jelly} with one colour and black jellies}

        In this section, we slightly modify the previous proof so as to prove the following result:

        \begin{theorem}
            \label{JellyPHard2}
            1-Colour \textsc{Jelly} is PSPACE-Hard when we allow the use of black jellies.
        \end{theorem}

        This result highlights the key role of black jellies in the complexity of 1-Colour \textsc{Jelly} since, in contrast, this very game without black jellies is known to be in NP. \cite{JellyNo}.



        \begin{proof}
        We use the same reduction as for the proof of Theorem \ref{JellyPHard} (unbounded number of colours) but reduce the number of colours used to one: this colour $c_1$ will be given to the jelly corresponding to edge $e$ and to all the jellies at the bottom of each vertex gadget. The other jellies, which represent the direction of the blue and red edges of $G$, are replaced with black jellies.

        Let $e = (u, v)$ the edge of $G$ for which we want to change the orientation, say from $(u, v)$ to $(v, u)$: that is to say, we want the level to be solvable even when the jelly corresponding to the flow in $e$ is in the vertex gadget representing $u$ and cannot leave to go back to $v$.

        We start by computing a visibility representation of $G$ such that $u$ is the leftmost vertex in the representation, which is possible for any edge\cite{Tamassia1986}.

        The goal is to add a solving zone at the bottom of the level and ensure all vertex jellies, as well as the jelly representing $e$, can access this solving zone and all merge into one jelly. This requires a few modifications in our gadgets: first in the vertex gadgets to ensure that the jellies can leave, then to make sure that they can cross a horizontal tunnel without being able to access other vertices. These modifications must not allow the jellies corresponding to the flows to leave their assigned gadgets and reach other parts of the level.

        See Figures \ref{oldGadget} and \ref{solv}. First, we add a gap of length 1 at the bottom of each vertex, leading to the solving zone at the bottom of the level. Then we modify the vertex jellies and the edge jellies as follows: first we multiply the width of the edge jellies by two without changing their height; this certifies that these jellies will not fall in the newly created gap, which has only length 1, while using the tunnels. Second, we remove the vertex jelly which is fixed inside the walls and we replace the other vertex jelly by one of height 2 and width 1. Thus, this jelly can leave the vertex gadget once it arrives at the gap, but it cannot use the tunnels of height 1 which are meant to be used only by the edge jellies.

        Last, there is an important change at the bottom of the vertex: the solving gap must now require two jellies of width 2 (now representing red edges) or one of width 4 (representing blue edges) to be completed. Simply modifying the width of the original solving gap would allow a single jelly of width 2 to be used as a moving platform across the gap, breaking the minimum inflow requirement. The new shape of the gap prevents this. 

		See Figure \ref{schema} for the mechanism to cross horizontal edges and bring the coloured jelly to the solving zone.

