    \subsection{General \textsc{Jelly} is in PSPACE}\label{sec:inPspace}

    In this section, and for the sake of completeness, we prove that \textsc{Jelly} with an unbounded number of colours and black jellies is in PSPACE. 

    \begin{theorem}
        \label{inPspace}
        \textsc{Jelly} is in PSPACE.
    \end{theorem}

    \begin{proof}
    Let M = (Q, $\Sigma$, $\Gamma$, $\delta$, q0, qf) be a non-deterministic Turing machine. Given input <J> of size n, M proceeds as follows: If <J> is not a level of \textsc{Jelly}, reject. Else, let J be the level of \textsc{Jelly} encoded by <J>. We consider the configuration graph of M: we represent each possible configuration of M in a directed graph such that there is a directed edge from configuration $u$ to $v$ if and only if $v$ is reachable from $u$ in only one step. Such a graph is exponential in n. 

    Solving J is equivalent to finding a path in the configuration graph between the initial configuration of the level and an accepting configuration. This corresponds to the graph reachability problem, which is known to be in NL in relation to the size of the graph. Observe that there is no need to explicitely draw the whole configuration graph, we only need to examine it state by state. 

    Therefore \textsc{Jelly} is in NPSPACE and, by Savitch's theorem, since NPSPACE = PSPACE, \textsc{Jelly} $\in$ PSPACE.

        This concludes the proof of Theorem \ref{inPspace}. 

    \end{proof}


