\subsection{The ``one, true, and natural'' tensor product}
In passing we take note  that there is a ``one, true, and natural'' tensor product space associated with an ordered pair of vector spaces $(\mathcal{V}_1,\mathcal{V}_2)$, each over the same field $K$.  The nature of the ``one, true and natural'' tensor product space is generally unsuitable unless it is isomorphically replaced by a more ``practical'' tensor product space to be used in modeling quantum systems.  There is unfortunately no natural such isomorphism, and we therefore lose the ``one, true, and natural'' aspect of the tensor product we now sketch when we replace it by something conceptually less involved.  More rigorous details are available in \cite{Ger85}.

Genroch's presentation gives a construction in which the tensor product space is a quotient space of the free vector space ${\sf Free}(\mathcal{V}_1 \cross \mathcal{V}_2)$ on the \textit{set} of elements of the ordered pair of \textit{sets} $\mathcal{V}_1$ and $\mathcal{V}_2$.  We stress that these are sets because  the vector space structure on $\mathcal{V}_1$ and $\mathcal{V}_2$ is ignored in constructing ${\sf Free}(\mathcal{V}_1 \cross \mathcal{V}_2)$ and then recovered when constructing $\mathcal{V}_1 \otimes \mathcal{V}_2$ as a quotient of the free space.   Let $\alpha$ be the function that maps a pair of elements $(v_1,v_2)$ to the function $\nu$ that maps $(v_1,v_2)$ to $1 \in K$ and all other elements of 
$(\mathcal{V}_1,\mathcal{V}_2)$ to $0 \in K$.  Then ${\sf Free}(\mathcal{V}_1 \cross \mathcal{V}_2)$ is the set of all functions from
$(v_1,v_2)$ to $K$ that have \textit{finite support}; i.e. functions that output $0$ for all but a finite set of inputs.  The vector space structure on $\mathcal{V}_1$ and $\mathcal{V}_2$ is effectively recovered as well as giving the ``one, true, and natural'' tensor product space  by taking a quotient ${\sf Free}(\mathcal{V}_1 \cross \mathcal{V}_2)/\mathcal{V}'$ for which the composition $\eta \circ \alpha$ is bilinear, where $\eta$ is the \textit{natural map} from ${\sf Free}(\mathcal{V}_1 \cross \mathcal{V}_2)$ onto ${\sf Free}(\mathcal{V}_1 \cross \mathcal{V}_2)/\mathcal{V}'$.  Informally, the subspace $\mathcal{V}'$ is the set of elements in ${\sf Free}(V_1 \cross V_2)$ which would have to map to $0$ if $\alpha$ were somehow to be bilinear, so we mod out that subspace.  What we have called the ``one, true, and natural'' tensor product operator is the bilinear $\eta \circ \alpha$.   
This construction can be augmented to obtain the tensor product space so as to satisfy the following identity where $S_1$ and $S_2$ are \textit{any} nonempty sets of any kind:
\begin{equation}
{\sf Free}(S_1 \cross S_2) = {\sf Free}(S_1) \otimes {\sf Free}(S_2) 
\end{equation}
We are interested in more mundane types such as 
