where a local projective measurement is made on qubit {\sf Q1} serves to characterize \textbf{local} projective measurements on what one part of a multipartite quantum system. The measurement can be seen as a joint measurement on both qubits with nothing acting on {\sf Q2}.  In terms of Postulate 3 the observable is the Kronecker product $M \otimes I$ where $M$ is an observable for a projective measurement of {\sf Q1} and $I$ is the identity observable for {\sf Q2}. (The great advantage of performing a measurement that does nothing is that it can be accomplished from a location arbitrarily remote from the physical site of the measurement.)   There arises the question: How much freedom is there to change the underlying tensor product operator and still maintain the locality of the measurement on {\sf Q1}?  

By lemma~(\ref{le:lem1}) any local unitary operation that produces tensor product operation $\otimes' = (W \otimes W')\circ \otimes$ where $W$  and $W'$ are unitary operators on the state space of {\sf Q1} and {\sf Q2}, respectively, will maintain, as it is to be expected, the locality of the measurement on {\sf Q1} since
\begin{equation}
M\otimes' I = (W \otimes W')(M\otimes' I)(W \otimes W')^\dagger = WMW^\dagger \otimes I.
\end{equation}

An observable for a nonlocal projective measurement with respect to a tensor product operation 
$\otimes$ may be an observable for a local measurement with respect to different tensor product operators $\otimes'$. 
