First, consider any two nonempty \textit{sets} $X$ and $Y$.   The cartesian product of these two sets is usually taken, \textit{incorrectly}, as the set of all \textit{ordered} pairs $(x,\,y)$ such that $x \in X$ and $y \in Y$.   The correct definition, seldom given outside of category theory textbooks, boils down for our purposes as follows: the elements of the cartesian product $X \cross Y$ is the set of functions $f\!\!: \{1,2\} \longrightarrow X \cup Y$ such that $f(1) \in X$ and $f(2) \in Y$.  The ordered pair $(u,v) \in X \cross Y$ is then seen as an abbreviation of 
$\{1\mapsto u,\,2\mapsto v\}$, or more succinctly as $(u_1,v_2)$, which is also $(v_2,u_1)$.  
$(v,u)$ would be and abbreviation of $\{1\mapsto v,\,2\mapsto u\}$ and thus in general 
$(u,v) \neq (v,u)$.   The practical effect of these considerations is that for the usual tensor product
notation, when we want to calculate a Kronecker product such as

