In the sequel we will drop the subscript $\bm{B}_0$ on matrix notation for qubit states.
In performing calculations with Kronecker products one must be careful to write matrices in the correct bases.  In particular, for three vectors
\begin{equation}
\begin{brmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \end{brmatrix} \in \mathcal{H}_{{\sf A}_1},\ \begin{brmatrix} y_1 \\ y_2 \end{brmatrix} \in \mathcal{H}_{{\sf A}_2},\ \begin{brmatrix} z_1 \\ z_2 \end{brmatrix}\!\!\! \in \mathcal{H}_{\sf B},
\end{equation}
if we were to calculate the tensor product of all three using the Kronecker product operator pair $\otimes_{(13)2}$ and $\otimes_2$, we would obtain
{\small
\begin{equation}
\left(\begin{brmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \end{brmatrix} \otimes_2 \begin{brmatrix} z_1 \\ z_2 \end{brmatrix}\right)
\otimes_{(13)2} \begin{brmatrix} y_1 \\ y_2 \end{brmatrix}
= 
\ \ \ \begin{brmatrix} x_1z_1 \\ x_1z_2 \\ x_2z_1 \\ x_2z_2 \end{brmatrix}_{\bm{B}_2}\!\!\!\!\!\otimes_{(13)2}
\begin{brmatrix} y_1 \\ y_2 \end{brmatrix} = 
\begin{brmatrix} x_1z_1y_1 \\ x_1z_1y_2 \\ x_1z_2y_1 \\ x_1z_2y_2 \\ x_2z_1y_1 \\
x_2z_1y_2 \\ x_2z_2y_1 \\ x_2z_2y_2 \end{brmatrix}_{\bm{B}}
\end{equation}
}
$\!\!\!$\\
which is the wrong answer! It is wrong because the second argument to $\otimes_{(13)2}$ should be between the first and third arguments. It is not a defect of tensor product/Kronecker product notation and explicitly is not a defect of $\otimes_{(13)2}$; it is a defect of infix notation, e.g. ${\sf arg}_1 \cdot {\sf arg}_2$, which doesn't allow for situations such as this, rather than prefix notation, e.g. $\cdot({\sf arg}_1,\, {\sf arg}_2)$, which does:  We could simply correctly express the action of the Kronecker product operator pair $\otimes_{(13)2}$ and $\otimes_2$ by simply defining the 3-ary operation $(\otimes_2, \otimes_{(13)2})$ by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:fuss}
\begin{array}{rl}
& (\otimes_2, \otimes_{(13)2})\left(\begin{brmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \end{brmatrix}, \begin{brmatrix} y_1 \\ y_2 \end{brmatrix}, \begin{brmatrix} z_1 \\ z_2 \end{brmatrix}\right) \\
= & \\
& \left(\!\!\left(\begin{brmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \end{brmatrix} \otimes_2 \begin{brmatrix} z_1 \\ z_2 \end{brmatrix}\right)_{\!\!\!\bm{B}_2}
\!\!\!\!\otimes_{(13)2} \begin{brmatrix} y_1 \\ y_2 \end{brmatrix}\right)_{\!\!\!\bm{B}}\!,% 
\\
\rule{0pt}{3ex} = & \mbox{(in the usual informal notation)} \\
& \rule{0pt}{4.5ex} \begin{brmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \end{brmatrix} \otimes_1 \begin{brmatrix} y_1 \\ y_2 \end{brmatrix} \otimes_{1} \begin{brmatrix} z_1 \\ z_2 \end{brmatrix}\,
\end{array}
\end{equation}
thereby clearing away all the fuss. The second equality in equation~(\ref{eq:fuss}) holds due to the definition of the action of $\otimes_{(13)2}$ on $\bm{B}_2 \cross \bm{B}_1$.

