allocation is optimized,  increasing the number of CB clusters  provides a
greater flexibility. In fact, in the system with $C=1$ CB cluster, we optimize up to $K$ power allocation coefficients. However, in the system with $C$ CB clusters, we can optimize the  sum SE over $CK$ transmit powers. Therefore, the solution space is expanding by increasing the number of   CB clusters.

% \item  CF-mMIMO with $C=1$ CB cluster, i.e.,  fully centralized beamforming design,  outperforms other cases with $C\geq 2$.  More specifically, as the number of CB clusters increases, sum SE decreases.  \comm{This is reasonable because, for larger $C$, we can only perform intra-cluster suppression, while inter-cluster interference degrades the overall performance.}

\item The sum-SE  performance gap between $C=M$  and $C=1$,  decreases with OPA. For example,  the sum-SE performance loss of $C=M$ case with respect to $C=1$ case is $75\%$ and $38 \%$ under EPA and OPA strategies, respectively.
