OSs manage a vast number of processes that continuously start and stop. These systems could benefit from analyzing historical execution behavior to optimize resource management, such as intelligently allocating resources based on usage patterns. However, OSs often rely on hard-coded heuristics that lack adaptability to changing usage scenarios \cite{schupbach2012tackling}. This highlights the need for mechanisms that can leverage the accumulated knowledge and patterns of user behavior to determine optimal configurations for operational timings, including thread scheduling, memory paging, and the frequency of buffer cache flushing. In this section, we will review the efforts documented in the literature that detail the application of ML methods to enhance OS functionalities. 
\subsection{Scheduling}

Scheduling, a core mechanism for OSs, balances fairness, responsiveness, and throughput by efficiently managing resources \cite{li2023batch,liu2022multi}. The effectiveness of a scheduler directly impacts overall system performance, influencing factors such as task latency, resource utilization, and user experience. However, in the context of increasingly complex hardware architectures and diverse software workloads, traditional OS schedulers, which rely heavily on static, heuristic-based algorithms, often struggle to adapt to dynamic and heterogeneous environments. These limitations manifest as inefficiencies in handling workload variability, hardware resource contention, and energy optimization. To address these challenges, recent research has explored the integration of ML techniques into OS scheduling. By leveraging data-driven models, ML-based schedulers aim to automate decision-making processes and transcend the constraints of traditional approaches. These adaptive methods enhance scheduling flexibility, improve prediction accuracy for workload behaviors, and optimize resource allocation in real-time, thus addressing the evolving demands of modern computing systems.

