\paragraph{RF(DT)}A decision tree is a type of ML model used when the relationship between a set of predictor variables and a response variable is non-linear,while random forest is essentially a collection of decision trees. It is quick to fit to a dataset and easy to interpret. Chowdhury et al. \cite{benabderrahmane2024hack} used RF as one if the models to  accurately detect the attack from the network traffic. To construct the random forest classifier, the authors employed the Random Forest Regressor, which serves as both a regressor and a meta estimator. It accomplishes this by fitting multiple decision trees to different subsets of the dataset. For this specific model, the authors opted for a forest containing 1000 trees. Ahmed et al. \cite{ahmed2022heterogeneous} used RF to build a device fitness model,based on the Dataset collected during runtime and statically.  De Wit et al. \cite{panman2022dynamic} trained a statistical classifier able to recognize malware signatures in any log data collected on a smartphone. The classifier was trained, cross-validated, and tested using the dataset described above and RF classifier had a better performance. Metzge el at. \cite{metzger2021device} used RF to get a optimal kernel runtime switching slice size. The model is a random forest regressor with 50 decision trees with a depth of two for the GPU model. Ongun et al. \cite{ongun2021living} used RF to  get the probability of a command being malicious,based on labels dataset. Sun et al. \cite{sun2021linux} used RF to filter storage I/O parameters demonstrates that tuning only the important parameters can nearly achieve the high performance obtained by tuning all parameters. Known for their versatility in handling non-linear relationships and minimal tuning requirements, RF/DT are used for tasks such as attack detection and configuration settings. They are chosen for their ability to quickly fit to datasets and provide interpretable models.

\paragraph{RNN(LSTM)}RNNs are a class of neural networks designed to handle sequential data. They have feedback connections, allowing them to maintain an internal state or memory. Each step in an RNN processes an input and updates its hidden state based on the current input and the previous hidden state. While LSTM, a type of RNN architecture with a more complex cell structure,were introduced to address the vanishing gradient problem. Motivated by the problem that exploiting 3D-stacking memory's performance is challenging because bandwidth utilization heavily depends on address mapping in the memory controller, Zhang et al. \cite{zhang2022software} used a software-defined address mapping, allowing user programs to directly control low-level memory hardware in an intelligent and fine-grained manner. LSTM is used to in a method to get access pattern information to select an address mapping. It identify the major variables that significantly contribute to external memory access and have a substantial impact on memory traffic and data movement. Miura et al. \cite{9355915} proposed to apply LSTM for time series data, for cache replacement in the second cache. Designed to handle sequential data, RNN/LSTM are particularly suited for tasks that require capturing temporal dependencies, such as managing memory access patterns in 3D-stacking memory systems.

\paragraph{KNN} KNN is a supervised ML algorithm used for both classification and regression tasks.
The fundamental idea behind KNN is simple: neighbors influence each other. If you’re surrounded by similar things, you’re likely similar too. It is widely applicable in pattern recognition, data mining, and intrusion detection. Yang et al. \cite{yang2022improvement} introduce a  KNN-based ML algorithms can accurately predict the Turnaround-time (TaT) of a process. It can effectively reduce the TaT of the process and reduce the number of process context switches.


\paragraph{MLP} An MLP is a type of feedforward neural network used for supervised learning tasks, such as classification and regression. Chen et al. \cite{chen2020machine} argues that traditional Linux CFS scheduler maximizes the utilization of processing time but overlooks the contention for lower-level hardware resources and try to solve the above problem using an ML-based resource-aware load balancer.They employed supervised imitation learning to replace a portion of its internal logic with an MLP model. This trained MLP model emulates the kernel’s load balancing decisions. MLP is chosen because this current work doesn’t require a very complex model and MLP has a relatively simple implementation compared to the other models. Based on this work,Qiu et al. \cite{imteaj2021survey} propose the concept of reconfigurable kernel datapaths that enables kernels to self-optimize dynamically to reduce the cost of kernel. The authors also used MLP ML model that can mimic Linux CFS decisions.


\section{Large Language Model Enhanced Operating Systems}

The rapid development of LLMs has introduced transformative capabilities in natural language understanding and generation, making them a promising tool for advancing operating system (OS) design and interaction. LLMs, such as GPT-4 \cite{achiam2023gpt}, LLaMA \cite{touvron2023llama}, and Gemini \cite{team2023gemini}, have demonstrated remarkable proficiency in processing and generating human-like text, understanding complex instructions, and adapting to a wide variety of tasks with minimal or no task-specific training. These models are built on advances in deep learning architectures, particularly Transformer-based designs, and trained on vast datasets that enable them to capture context, semantics, and nuanced relationships between concepts.

One of the defining features of LLMs is their ability to act as general-purpose reasoning and interaction tools. Unlike traditional machine learning models, which are often task-specific, LLMs are highly adaptable, capable of handling a broad range of tasks through natural language interfaces. Their ability to understand intent, provide detailed explanations, and generate contextualized responses makes them an ideal complement to OS functionalities. As operating systems serve as the backbone for user-computer interaction, integrating LLMs can enable more intuitive, conversational interfaces, where users can communicate their needs and preferences in natural language rather than relying on rigid command structures or graphical user interfaces. The integration of LLMs into OSs presents a significant opportunity to enhance the user experience and overall system functionality  \cite{chan2023consistency,wu2024copilot}. LLMs can transform the way users interact with and manage their computing environments \cite{hè2024perospersonalizedselfadaptingoperating}. 





\subsection{Development}

LLMs possess powerful natural language processing capabilities, making them highly effective at understanding historical documentation on human experiences with operating systems. This ability is particularly valuable in the era of the Internet of Everything, where engineers invest significant effort in adapting hardware and applications to achieve optimal resource utilization and efficiency.

Kamath et al.'s LLaMaS \cite{kamath2024herding} and Chen et al.'s AutoOS \cite{chen2024autoos} both utilize LLMs to address challenges in operating system configuration optimization, but they focus on different domains and employ distinct methodologies. LLaMaS addresses the complexities of adapting to diverse hardware environments by using LLMs to interpret plain text hardware specifications and recommend optimized OS strategies. Its architecture comprises a frontend and a backend: the frontend deciphers system characteristics and converts them into actionable embeddings, which the backend then uses for real-time OS decisions, such as managing memory tier data movement. In an experiment, LLaMaS demonstrated its capability by using ChatGPT to dynamically adjust memory allocation for CPU and GPU tasks based on workload demands. By leveraging LLMs' zero-shot learning capabilities, LLaMaS eliminates the need for manual adjustments or device-specific coding, significantly reducing administrative and development costs. In contrast, AutoOS tackles the challenge of Linux kernel configuration optimization, a critical task for AIoT devices with constrained hardware resources. AutoOS models the kernel configuration space as a dynamic tree structure and employs a state-machine-based framework to traverse, prune, propose, and correct configuration options. The LLM frontend identifies performance-critical options, while the backend applies these refinements to boost system performance. Experiments showed that AutoOS could enhance UnixBench scores by up to 25\% compared to vendor-provided defaults, achieving superior results with minimal human input. By automating the debugging and refinement of kernel configurations, AutoOS reduces the time and expertise required for manual optimization, addressing the complexity of configuring over 15,000 kernel options.

Both LLaMaS and AutoOS leverage LLMs to simplify traditionally complex OS tasks, minimizing the need for manual tuning and expert knowledge. They also demonstrate the ability of LLMs to adapt dynamically to changing requirements. Moreover, both frameworks utilize LLMs' reasoning and zero-shot learning capabilities to autonomously make decisions, showcasing their effectiveness in reducing development and administrative overhead.


Another important and obvious area where LLMs excel is coding. As a type of textual data, code can be considered another form of human language, making LLMs particularly well-suited for tasks involving programming. This highlights the crucial role LLMs can play in improving software development processes, from code generation to debugging and vulnerability repair. For instance, SecRepair \cite{islam2024llmpoweredcodevulnerabilityrepair} is a system specifically designed to address the challenge of identifying and repairing code vulnerabilities. Powered by an LLM, it integrates reinforcement learning and a semantic reward mechanism to enhance its performance. To support its training, the authors have compiled and released a comprehensive instruction-based vulnerability dataset, which serves as a key resource for building a robust model for vulnerability analysis. Furthermore, SecRepair employs a reinforcement learning technique with semantic rewards to generate concise and contextually appropriate code commit comments. This approach, inspired by how humans resolve code issues, not only automates the repair process but also provides developers with a clear understanding of the vulnerabilities and their resolutions, ultimately streamlining software development and improving code security.

\paragraph{Insights and Challenges:} LLMs have the potential to significantly enhance software development processes, including tasks such as code generation, debugging, and vulnerability detection, by reducing manual effort and improving overall efficiency. The development and release of comprehensive, instruction-based datasets are essential for training models capable of addressing complex tasks like vulnerability analysis and repair. However, several challenges remain. For instance, mainstream operating systems like Linux consist of vast codebases with deeply coupled structures, making it difficult for current LLMs to fully capture all necessary modifications when proposing adaptations or optimizations. While LLMs can provide efficient suggestions, they may overlook related areas in the code that also require changes. Additionally, training a new LLM model demands significant time and computational resources, posing challenges in scenarios where engineers frequently switch to new hardware platforms. It is often impractical and uneconomical to train a fresh model for each new hardware adaptation, highlighting the need for more efficient and scalable solutions.


\subsection{User Interaction}

LLMs are transforming user interaction by enabling more natural, intelligent, and personalized experiences across various systems. As the critical bridge between hardware and software, the operating system plays a pivotal role in shaping the user interaction experience, which in turn defines how users engage with and perceive technology. Researchers are now striving to bring this revolutionary shift into the realm of operating systems, aiming to redefine how users interact with computing environments.

Personalized services can meet specific user needs, enhancing satisfaction and loyalty. Furthermore, interactive systems can understand users' perspectives and needs through well-designed interfaces and experiences. Zhang et al. \cite{zhang2024enhanced}  explores the potential applications of large language models, ML, and interaction design in recommendation systems and operating systems. By integrating these technologies, more intelligent and personalized services can be provided, meeting user needs and promoting continuous improvement and optimization of products, which holds great value for both recommendation research and user experience applications.


Some researchers are exploring deeper structural reforms in OSs through the integration of LLMs. The AIOS-Agent ecosystem \cite{ge2023llm,mei2024aios} positions large language models (LLMs) as an "operating system with a soul," embedding them into the OS kernel to enable intelligent decision-making and resource management in Figure.\ref{fig:aiosarc}. The LLM's context window functions as memory, handling relevant data, while external storage acts as a file system with advanced retrieval capabilities. Hardware and software are treated as peripherals and libraries, facilitating seamless agent-environment interaction. Natural language serves as the primary programming interface, democratizing software development. This ecosystem supports both single- and multi-agent applications capable of executing a wide range of tasks. Building on the AIOS concept, various studies have further enriched the ecosystem with additional components.
