Recent breakthroughs in LLM-based multi-agent systems, building on the success of single LLMs as unified decision-makers, have unlocked new horizons in complex problem-solving and environment simulation. This progress signals the potential for multi-agent LLMs to revolutionize OS development. Studies like \cite{qian2023communicative} and \cite{hong2023metagpt} showcase multi-agent LLM models' efficacy in software development, from analysis to debugging.

In the domain of OS development, the integration of multi-agent systems as shown in Figure \ref{fig:ma-llm}, facilitates a specialized, collaborative, and adaptable environment. A constellation of agents, each dedicated to specific niches such as kernel architecture, driver engineering, or cybersecurity, collectively contributes to enhanced project productivity\cite{yang2023kernelgptenhancedkernelfuzzing,zheng2023kenkernelextensionsusing,alrashedy2024llmspatchsecurityissues,xing2023promptsapperllmempoweredsoftware}. These agents facilitate knowledge exchange and consensus-building akin to human collaboration. They concurrently tackle OS components, recalibrate in response to evolving project dynamics, and oversee integration, testing, and debugging phases, proactively addressing potential issues.Inherently adaptable, the system reassigns agent responsibilities or integrates fresh entities to align with technological advancements, market dynamics, or emerging security vulnerabilities, ensuring continuous OS refinement. Certain agents conduct simulations to assess OS performance under varied scenarios, supplying empirical insights to inform judicious decision-making, preserving the OS’s technological relevance and competitiveness.


\subsection{LLM Based Intelligent OS} 
Ideas such as those presented in \cite{mei2024aios} and \cite{jia2024agent} propose innovative frameworks for current LLM-based operating systems or runtimes. However, in line with our definition of an AI-driven OS, we believe that the generalization capabilities of LLMs position them to function as various sub-modules of operating systems—such as schedulers or filesystems—effectively replacing traditional OS components. This approach can be realized without necessitating changes to existing applications or hardware adaptations, allowing for a smoother transition and integration. By leveraging the adaptive and conversational nature of LLMs, these systems could enhance resource management, task scheduling, and data handling in a more intuitive and efficient manner, ultimately transforming the landscape of operating systems while maintaining compatibility with current software and hardware infrastructures. Future research in the domain of LLMs integrated as OS will focus on following areas. 

\paragraph{Platform-Agnostic Adaptation} LLMs' inherent understanding of natural language empowers them to interpret a spectrum of system artifacts, from logs to hardware specs, irrespective of software platforms or hardware architectures. This capability facilitates dynamic OS parameter adjustment and context-aware optimization strategies, ensuring the system stays in sync with hardware advancements and performs optimally across diverse ecosystems.

\paragraph{Intelligent Resource Allocation} Leveraging pattern recognition and predictive analytics, LLMs can make informed real-time resource management decisions, preemptively addressing resource demands and workload fluctuations. This proactive approach optimizes system performance, reduces latency, and maximizes resource utilization.

\paragraph{Natural Language Interface} Integrating LLMs into the OS interface enables a conversational interaction model, replacing traditional interfaces with intuitive text-based requests. This simplifies user engagement and broadens accessibility, enhancing user experience and lowering the learning curve.

\paragraph{Customized User Experience} LLMs learn from user behavior and preferences, dynamically adjusting the OS interface and functionality to cater to individual needs. This personalization fosters a more engaging and productive user interface, tailored to unique usage patterns.

\paragraph{Instructions Interporation} Traditional operating systems link to applications through system calls, and if large models can accurately translate and understand these instructions, they indeed have the potential to replace the system call layer or even the entire operating system. By leveraging natural language processing and contextual understanding, large models could directly interpret application demands and generate corresponding system-level operations, thereby simplifying the interaction between users and the system. Such a replacement could enhance the system's flexibility and usability while lowering the technical requirements for developers and users.


