Despite this setup, \textit{ts-net} was the only method to consistently achieve scores no lower than $0.790$ across all metrics and datasets. Moreover, it achieved the best performance in $11$ out of the $21$ evaluation cases, outperforming all other methods; the second-best method, \textit{deg-cd}, ranked first in $9$ cases. \textit{ts-net} obtained the highest $T_{val}$ score on five out of eight networks and consistently ranked among the top three in the remaining metrics. Its performance was especially pronounced on \textit{timik}, demonstrating that predicting local rankings to improve memory efficiency does not compromise predictive quality. Notable performance deviations were observed only on the \textit{arxiv} and \textit{eu-trans} networks, both of which have considerably more layers ($13$ and $37$, respectively) than the maximum of $5$ layers seen in the training data.

Among the other methods, \textit{deg-cd} and \textit{deg-c} yielded stable and competitive performance, especially on smaller networks, where they frequently ranked in the top three. However, they underperformed on sparser or more irregular graphs such as \textit{timik}. The results of \textit{nghb-s} and \textit{nghb-sd} were mixed: while effective in $T_{val}$ on \textit{arxiv}, \textit{ckmp}, and \textit{lazega}, their scores in $S_{val}$ and $F_{auc}$ were inconsistent. The \textit{random} baseline predictably placed last in most cases. \textit{mn2v-km} performed reasonably on \textit{ckmp} and \textit{timik}, but fell behind top methods overall. Finally, \textit{DeepIM} performed poorly with exception for $T_{val}$ on the small \textit{l2-course} network and failed to run on the largest graph (\textit{timik}) due to memory issues.

\section{Conclusions}

This study addresses the problem of identifying super-spreaders in multilayer networks by proposing a novel, generalisable solution. We introduced \textit{TopSpreadersNetwork}, an inductive graph neural network capable of processing multilayer networks of varying sizes and complexities. As part of this work, we also constructed a dedicated dataset: \textit{TopSpreadersDataset}, which captures actor-level diffusion characteristics under the Multilayer Independent Cascade Model. Finally, we proposed and validated alternative techniques for data transformation and embedding fusion that improve the robustness of ranking-learning task from multirelational data.

Despite these contributions, some limitations remain. While multilayer networks constitute a powerful and flexible framework, they are still a subset of the broader class of heterogeneous graphs, which are not directly addressed in this work. Moreover, although \textit{TopSpreadersNetwork} demonstrates superior performance in our evaluations, relatively simple heuristics such as Degree Centrality continue to yield strong results. The main advantage of our approach lies in its interpretability: rather than producing scores in a black-box fashion, it first predicts a vector of four interpretable features, which can then be used to construct tailored rankings. This offers flexibility for use cases where, for instance, actors causing the highest peak of infections are more desirable than those triggering longer diffusions, something heuristic or end-to-end solutions do not easily allow.

Several promising directions exist for future research. First, the dataset may be extended in two independent ways. One option is to include diffusion results for small seed sets, generated using heuristics such as the greedy algorithm, thereby extending the learning task from scoring individual actors to evaluating entire seed sets. Another avenue is to reforge the dataset into a general benchmark for super-spreader identification, encompassing a broader range of diffusion models and network topologies. % In parallel, exploratory studies could be conducted to examine whether certain actors consistently emerge as super-spreaders across different spreading models, shedding light on deeper structural determinants of influence in complex networks.

