The results based on the performance evaluation of the various clustering algorithms are evaluated based on the Silhouette Scores and Davies-Bouldin Index shown in Table~\ref{tab:comparesilhouette} are as follows:
%\textit{\uline{t-SNE, UMPA, and PCA Evaluation:}}
t-SNE generally has higher silhouette scores than UMAP across most clustering algorithms. PCA shows the lowest values. Similarly, t-SNE exhibits the lowest DBI, indicating better cluster separation, followed by UMAP and PCA. t-SNE shows the best performance in both silhouette scores and DBI for most algorithms, suggesting it creates better-separated clusters. It can be noted that t-SNE is slightly better suited for algorithms sensitive to cluster shapes, like Spectral Clustering and MB K-means. UMAP consistently achieves moderately high silhouette scores and DBI values, suggesting decent cluster separation. It performs comparably to t-SNE for algorithms like Spectral Clustering but struggles more with HDBSCAN and LDA. On the contrary, PCA generally shows the lowest silhouette scores and higher DBI, indicating poor cluster separation compared to t-SNE and UMAP.
Agglomerative clustering on PCA is an exception, showing relatively strong performance.

%\textit{\uline{Clutering Algorithms Evaluation:}}
Overall, \textit{Min-Batch K-means } achieved the best silhouette score (t-SNE (0.4589), followed by UMAP (0.4119) and PCA (0.3712)) and BDI (t-SNE (0.6623), followed by UMAP (0.7206), and PCA (0.7801)). All the other clustering algorithms also show a similar trend. 
Since DBSCAN showed limited effectiveness in identifying only one or two clusters, we did not include the results. 

Based on the detailed evaluation, t-SNE and UMAP prove more effective for visualizing and clustering PP data compared to PCA, as they better capture the \textit{non-linear relationships} and complex structures within the data. Among the clustering algorithms, MB K-Means shows consistently moderate to good performance across different embeddings, which means it is a reliable choice for PP data analysis. Agglomerative clustering generally performs well, particularly with t-SNE and UMAP embeddings, indicating its suitability with the right parameter tuning. HDBSCAN and Spectral clustering underperform across all types of embeddings used in the study, indicating that these methods may not be useful for this specific dataset or require significant tuning to yield meaningful results. 

The findings in this article support Research Question 1 (RQ1) by establishing a foundation for transforming privacy policies into interactive and user-friendly visualizations. Additionally, they support Research Question 2 (RQ2) by utilizing graph mining algorithms to uncover the structural properties of semantic clusters, which allows for a deeper analysis of PPs. Together, these insights enhance transparency and help identify key themes within PP data.
%The visualization presented in Fig. \ref{fig:minibatch} shows the semantic clustering of graph nodes using MB K-Means applied to embeddings reduced via t-SNE. Nodes are grouped into five semantically meaningful themes of clusters: (i) User Activity, (ii) Device Information, (iii) Media and Location, (iv) Device and Advertising, and (v) Unique Device Identification, where each cluster reflects a distinct thematic category, capturing related concepts within the graph. For example, the \textit{User Activity} cluster includes nodes such as ``information you register" and ``photograph you upload," while the Device Information cluster comprises nodes like ``ip address" and ``version." Node labels are annotated directly, and clusters are color-coded for clarity, with semantic themes assigned based on centroid similarity to predefined categories. The semantic aspect is reinforced by mapping cluster centroids to predefined themes using \textit{cosine similarity}~\cite{cosine2024} of embeddings. This visualization emphasizes the interpretability of graph-based data by revealing thematic patterns and relationships through semantic clustering.
%\subsection{Suitability of Graph-Based Techniques for Digital Forensics Investigations}
%This study's potential uses in digital forensics, namely in the analysis and interpretation of PPs to aid investigations and regulatory compliance, make it significant. Crucial information regarding data collection, sharing, and usage practices is frequently included in PPs. This information is essential for determining if an organization complies with legal and ethical standards. This study offers forensic investigators a novel way to find hidden patterns, correlations, and contradictions in these documents by utilizing graph-based representations and clustering algorithms. Knowledge graph-generated visualizations, for instance, might draw attention to differences between declared and observed practices, helping investigators spot any infractions of data protection regulations like CCPA or GDPR. \\
%Furthermore, the automatic extraction of privacy-relevant clauses is made possible by the combination of ML and NLP, greatly cutting down on the time and effort needed to analyze lengthy and complicated policies manually. In legal battles or regulatory audits, when prompt access to organized, actionable findings might impact case results, this capacity is very beneficial. Investigators may benchmark regulations and identify anomalies suggestive of malicious intent or non-compliance according to the clustering techniques employed in this study, which also highlight patterns in privacy practices across several firms. \\
Overall, this study lays the groundwork for integrating these methods into forensic procedures while also advancing the field of PP research. The suggested techniques provide investigators the opportunity to ensure accountability, defend user rights, and promote confidence in digital ecosystems by increasing openness and facilitating a methodical assessment of PPs.
\section{Conclusion and Future Work}
%Summary of Key Findings and Contributions
%Limitations and Future Research Directions
%Practical Implications and Potential Applications

%For example, techniques like Named Entity Recognition (NER) and topic modeling are widely used to identify specific clauses or practices within privacy policies. Tools such as spaCy and BERT-based models can automatically classify and tag key privacy concepts.

This study examines the use of graph-based clustering techniques to analyze PPs, focusing on user activity tracking, device information sharing, and third-party data disclosures. By applying dimensionality reduction methods such as t-SNE, UMAP, and PCA, different clustering approaches were evaluated for their effectiveness in identifying patterns within PPs. The visualizations and analyses demonstrate how clustering and graph-based techniques can simplify complex PPs by grouping related terms into semantically meaningful categories, such as user activity or device information (RQ1). These clusters, generated using algorithms like MB K-Means and HDBSCAN, highlight thematic communities and relationships between terms, providing structural insights into the policies (RQ2). %Dimensionality reduction methods, such as t-SNE, UMAP, and PCA, further enhance the clarity of these groupings, while graph metrics like degree centrality identify key terms and their significance within the network (RQ2). Together, these methods contribute to improved transparency and accessibility for non-expert users by visually simplifying policy structures and uncovering hidden patterns within their data (RQ1).
The results suggest that these methods can support forensic experts and regulatory bodies in comparing policies, identifying variations, and assessing potential compliance issues.

Future research could refine this approach by integrating more advanced natural language processing techniques for improved entity recognition and context analysis. Expanding the dataset to include privacy policies from various industries may help assess the generalizability of these techniques. Additionally, developing automated tools that incorporate interactive visualizations could enhance the usability of PP analysis for both researchers and practitioners. These directions may contribute to improving transparency in PP interpretation and supporting investigations into data governance practices.

%The study evaluates the performance of various clustering algorithms applied to graph representations of privacy policies using dimensionality reduction techniques (t-SNE, UMAP, and PCA). The clustering results are assessed with two key metrics: Silhouette Scores and Davies-Bouldin Index. This study provides guidance for practitioners aiming to cluster graph-based representations of text, such as privacy policies: Use t-SNE or UMAP for dimensionality reduction when clustering complex data; Opt for Spectral Clustering or MB K-means for clustering algorithms, as they deliver superior results in preserving cluster structure; and avoid relying solely on PCA or algorithms like LDA for clustering in datasets with non-linear relationships or high complexity.

%This study helps in dissecting complex legal documents into actionable insights and underpins the development of more user-centric and transparent privacy policies. By leveraging graph-based analysis, companies can better navigate the evolving regulatory landscape, enhance their data-handling practices, and build stronger trust with their users. This approach also sets a foundation for automated, real-time PP analysis tools that could significantly impact future privacy governance and compliance monitoring. Future research could expand upon this methodology to include more companies and possibly automate aspects of the analysis to handle real-time policy updates, ensuring ongoing compliance and transparency.

