\subsection{Ablation Study}


The ablation study on the Argoverse 1 validation set systematically evaluates performance by incrementally adding each component. 
% The inclusion of MSE, LTAA, and LPRM consistently improves prediction accuracy, as shown by the reductions in minADE, minFDE, and MR in Table \ref{tab:ablation}. 
The inclusion of MSE, LTAA, and LPRM consistently and significantly improves prediction accuracy, as evidenced by the progressive reductions in minADE, minFDE, and MR, as shown in Table \ref{tab:ablation}. 
Model $A_1$ (Base+MSE) shows improvements over the base model $A_0$ (HiVT-64), with further gains observed when adding LTAA in model $A_2$ (Base+MSE+LTAA). Finally, model $A_3$ (Base+MSE+LTAA+LPRM), which includes all components, achieves the best performance, with the lowest minADE (0.661), minFDE (0.945), and MR (0.082), highlighting the effectiveness of the full LTMSformer framework. 

The visualization results from the ablation experiments are illustrated in Fig. \ref{fig:4}. Initially, in $A_0$, the predicted trajectories deviate significantly from the lane and even exceed its boundaries. Introducing the MSE in $A_1$ leads to noticeably improved prediction results, with trajectories closer to the ground truth and a substantial reduction in endpoint errors, achieving fewer predictions that exceed the lane boundaries. This demonstrates the critical role of the MSE module in capturing motion state attributes by encoding the motion state attributes of agents. Next, $A_2$ incorporates LTAA, ensuring that all predicted trajectories stay within lane boundaries while aligning trajectory trends more closely with the ground truth. This demonstrates LTAA's capability to capture motion trends in the temporal dimension. Finally, $A_3$ integrates the LPRM based on $A_2$, which not only ensures that all predicted trajectories stay within lane boundaries but also minimizes the overall error between the predictions and the ground truth. This highlights the essential role of LPRM in refining trajectory proposals and enhancing temporal-spatial consistency. In summary, the gradual incorporation of MSE, LTAA, and LPRM consistently enhances model performance in dynamic traffic interaction scenarios, as demonstrated by both qualitative and quantitative analyses.




% We conduct an ablation study to evaluate the contributions of LTAA, MSE, and LPRM, as shown in Table \ref{tab:ablation}. Compared to the baseline model HiVT-64 ($A_0$), both LTAA ($A_1$) and MSE ($A_2$) modules significantly improve performance when added to HiVT-64. Notably, when the LPRM module is introduced ($A_3$), minADE decreases from 0.687 to 0.668, minFDE drops from 1.030 to 0.953, and MR reduces from 0.103 to 0.085, demonstrating the most substantial performance improvement. The ablation study visualizations are shown in Fig. \ref{fig:4}. $A_0$ shows significant trajectory deviation and unreasonable turning radius. However, $A_1$ demonstrates that motion state attributes help to smooth the trajectory, ensuring it adheres more closely to the expected path. Moreover, $A_2$ captures more realistic turning behavior, with the trajectories becoming better aligned with the road structure, reducing unnecessary deviations and improving the accuracy of trajectory prediction. $A_3$ achieves the most significant improvement, as the predicted trajectories are highly accurate, closely following the road geometry with minimal deviations. This highlights the crucial role of LPRM in refining trajectory proposals and ensuring better temporal-spatial consistency. These results demonstrate the importance of each module (LTAA, MSE, LPRM) in improving both the accuracy and efficiency of trajectory prediction.

