\end{figure}

In a manner similar to that observed in the network size experiment presented in Section~\ref{subsec:size}, the curves of $c$ were subdivided into two major groups, corresponding to situations in which the respective values increase (group $A$) or decrease (group $B$) in mostly monotonical manner with the number of edges. However, unlike in that experiment, some curves contained a mixture of increasing or decreasing portions, which appeared smoothly or in oscillating and jagged manner. These cases have been understood as corresponding to group $C$. No curve remained nearly constant as the edges were progressively removed. Interestingly, the curves undergoing a monotonical decrease tended to be more linear that the other types of observed curves.

Figures~\ref{fig:barplot_remove}(a-c) present, respectively, the overall variation index $\psi$, the Pearson correlation coefficients, and the overall absolute magnitudes of the non-standardized measurement changes obtained for the edge removal experiment.

