\begin{abstract}
    The hardcore model is one of the most classic and widely studied examples of undirected graphical models. Given a graph $G$, the hardcore model describes a Gibbs distribution of $\lambda$-weighted independent sets of $G$. In the last two decades, a beautiful computational phase transition has been established at a precise threshold $\lambda_c(\Delta)$ where $\Delta$ denotes the maximum degree, where the task of sampling independent sets transfers from polynomial-time solvable to computationally intractable. 
    We study the critical hardcore model where $\lambda = \lambda_c(\Delta)$ and show that the Glauber dynamics, a simple yet popular Markov chain algorithm, mixes in $\tilde{O}(n^{7.44 + O(1/\Delta)})$ time on any $n$-vertex graph of maximum degree $\Delta\geq3$, significantly improving the previous upper bound $\tilde{O}(n^{12.88+O(1/\Delta)})$ by the recent work \cite{CCYZ24}.
    The core property we establish in this work is that the critical hardcore model is $O(\sqrt{n})$-spectrally independent, improving the trivial bound of $n$ and matching the critical behavior of the Ising model. Our proof approach utilizes an online decision-making framework to study a site percolation model on the infinite $(\Delta-1)$-ary tree, which can be interesting by itself. 
\end{abstract}


\newpage

\setcounter{page}{1}

\section{Introduction}

The \emph{hardcore model} is one of the most fundamental undirected graphical models that has been extensively studied in statistical physics, social science, probability theory, combinatorics, and computer science.

Given a graph $G=(V,E)$, we let $\II(G)$ denote the collection of all independent sets of $G$, where we recall that an independent set is a subset of vertices inducing no edges. 
The \emph{Gibbs distribution} $\mu_{G,\lambda}$ associated with the hardcore model on $G$ is parameterized by a vertex weight $\lambda > 0$ called the \emph{fugacity}. 
Each independent set $\sigma \in \II(G)$ receives a probability density given by
\begin{align*}
    \mu_{G,\lambda}(\sigma) = \frac{\lambda^{|\sigma|}}{Z_{G,\lambda}},
\end{align*}
where $Z_{G,\lambda}$ is a normalizing constant call the \emph{partition function} and is defined as
\begin{align*}
    Z_{G,\lambda} = \sum_{\sigma \in \II(G)} \lambda^{|\sigma|}.
\end{align*}

Perhaps the most amazing property of the hardcore model is the phase transition phenomenon associated with it. In fact, the hardcore model was originally proposed by statistical physicists to study and understand the phase transition in systems of hardcore gas particles. 
Let $\Delta \ge 3$ denote the maximum degree of the underlying graph.
The tree-uniqueness threshold $\lambda_c(\Delta) := \frac{(\Delta-1)^{\Delta-1}}{(\Delta-2)^\Delta}$ characterizes the uniqueness of the hardcore Gibbs measure on the infinite $\Delta$-regular tree. Furthermore, it also describes the existence of long-range correlations. 
Let each vertex be associated with a Bernoulli random variable, called the \emph{spin}, indicating whether the vertex is \emph{occupied} (i.e., included in the independent set) or \emph{unoccupied} (i.e., not included in the independent set).
Then, for small fugacity $\lambda \le \lambda_c(\Delta)$ the configuration at distance $\ell$ from the root has a vanishing influence on the root as $\ell$ tends to infinity, while for large fugacity $\lambda > \lambda_c(\Delta)$ the correlation is always bounded away from zero.

In the past two decades, a beautiful computational phase transition has been fully established for the problem of sampling from the hardcore model on graphs of maximum degree $\Delta$, precisely around the uniqueness threshold $\lambda_c(\Delta)$.
For $\lambda < \lambda_c(\Delta)$, there exist deterministic approximate counting algorithms for estimating the partition function \cite{Wei06,Bar06,PR19}, which in turn gives approximate samplers via standard reduction. 
Meanwhile, for $\lambda > \lambda_c(\Delta)$, no polynomial-time approximate counting and sampling algorithms exist assuming $\textsf{RP} \neq \textsf{NP}$ \cite{Sly10,Sly12,GSV16}.

While all deterministic approximate counting algorithms run in polynomial time, they suffer from a pretty slow runtime with the exponent of $n$ depending on the maximum degree and the slackness of the fugacity to the critical threshold.
In practice, Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) algorithms provide a simpler and significantly faster method for generating random samples from high-dimensional distributions, including the hardcore model studied in this work. 
Among them, the \emph{Glauber dynamics} (also known as the \emph{Gibbs sampler}) is one of the most important and popular examples.
The Glauber dynamics performs a random walk in the space $\II(G)$ of independent sets and, in each step, either stays the same or moves to an adjacent set whose Hamming distance to the current set is 1.
More specifically, from the current independent set $\sigma_t \in \II(G)$, the algorithm picks a vertex $v \in V$ uniformly at random and updates its spin: Let $S = \sigma_t \setminus \{v\}$; if $S \cup \{v\} \notin \II(G)$ then set $\sigma_{t+1} = S = \sigma_t$; otherwise, set $\sigma_{t+1} = S \cup \{v\}$ with probability $\lambda/(1+\lambda)$ and, mutually exclusively, set $\sigma_{t+1} = S$ with probability $1/(1+\lambda)$.

Let $P_{\mathrm{GD}}$ denote the transition matrix of the Glauber dynamics.
From basic Markov chain theories it is easy to show that the Glauber dynamics $P_{\mathrm{GD}}$ is irreducible, aperiodic, and reversible with respect to the Gibbs distribution $\mu_{G,\lambda}$, which is the uniqueness stationary distribution (i.e., $\mu_{G,\lambda} P_{\mathrm{GD}} = \mu_{G,\lambda}$).
The mixing time of Glauber dynamics is defined as
\begin{align*}
    T_{\mathrm{mix}}(P) = \max_{\sigma_0 \in \II(G)} \min_{t \in \Z_{\geq0}} \left\{ \TV{P^t(\sigma_0, \cdot)}{\mu_{G,\lambda}} \le \frac{1}{4} \right\},
\end{align*}
where $\sigma_0$ is the initial independent set, $P^t(\sigma_0, \cdot)$ is the distribution of the chain after $t$ steps when starting from $\sigma_0$, and $d_{\mathrm{TV}}(\cdot,\cdot)$ denotes the total variation distance.


In the past years, exciting progress has been made in understanding the mixing time of Glauber dynamics for the hardcore model. 
Anari, Liu, and Oveis Gharan introduced a highly powerful technique known as \emph{spectral independence} \cite{ALO20}, leading to significant advancements in this area, including resolutions to major open problems regarding mixing properties.
We refer to \cite{Liu23,SV23} for a thorough introduction of this technique.
In the subcritical regime (i.e., $\lambda < \lambda_c(\Delta)$), the mixing time of the Glauber dynamics was shown to be nearly linear $O(n \log n)$ \cite{ALO20,CLV21,CFYZ22,CE22}. 
Meanwhile, it was long known that in the supercritical regime (i.e., $\lambda > \lambda_c(\Delta)$), the mixing time could be exponentially large $\exp(\Omega(n))$ as witnessed by random $\Delta$-regular bipartite graphs \cite{MWW09}.

In a very recent work \cite{CCYZ24}, the mixing property is further investigated at the critical point (i.e., $\lambda = \lambda_c(\Delta)$).
For the upper bound, the mixing time of Glauber dynamics is $\tilde{O}(n^{2+4\e+O(1/\Delta)})$ on any $n$-vertex graph of maximum degree $\Delta$. 
For the lower bound, there exists an infinite sequence of graphs such that the mixing time is $\Omega(n^{4/3})$, which is, in particular, super-linear. 

In this work, we present an improved mixing time upper bound for the Glauber dynamics on the critical hardcore model.



\begin{theorem}
\label{thm:main}
    Let $\alpha \ge 0$ be a constant. 
    For any $n$-vertex graph $G=(V,E)$ of maximum degree $\Delta \ge 3$, the Glauber dynamics for the hardcore model on $G$ with fugacity $\lambda \le (1+\frac{\alpha}{\sqrt{n}}) \lambda_c(\Delta)$ satisfies
    \begin{align*}
        T_{\mathrm{mix}}(P_{\mathrm{GD}}) =  O_\alpha\left( n^{2+2\e+\frac{2\e}{\Delta-2}} \log \Delta\right).
    \end{align*}
\end{theorem}

Our upper bound scales as $\tilde{O}(n^{7.44 + O(1/\Delta)})$, significantly improving the $\tilde{O}(n^{12.88+O(1/\Delta)})$ mixing time established in \cite{CCYZ24}.

Similar to \cite{CCYZ24}, \cref{thm:main} is established via the spectral independence framework.
Our main contribution is to show that the critical hardcore model satisfies spectral independence of order $O(\sqrt{n})$, improving the trivial bound of $n$ used in \cite{CCYZ24}.
We show this new spectral independence result in a novel way by studying an online decision-making problem, which allows us to understand a site percolation model on the infinite tree, from which spectral independence readily follows.
We provide an overview of the necessary background and known results on spectral independence, as well as our new contribution and proof approach in \cref{sec:overview}.

\section{Proof Overview}
\label{sec:overview}

\subsection{Notations and definitions}
Denote the set of non-negative integers by $\Z_{\geq0}$, and the set of positive integers by $\Z^+$.
For any integers $a,b\in\Z$, define $a \wedge b$ by the minimum of $a$ and $b$, i.e, $a \wedge b := \min\{a,b\}$.

Let $\-{Ber}(p)$ denote the Bernoulli distribution with success probability $p\in[0,1]$.
Let $\-{Bin}(n,p)$ denote the binomial distribution with number of trails $n\in\Z^+$ and success probability $p\in[0,1]$.
Let $d_{\mathrm{TV}}(\cdot,\cdot)$ denote the total variation distance.
For any random variables $X,Y$, let $X\overset{d}{=}Y$ denote that $X$ and $Y$ are equal in distribution.

Let $G=(V,E)$ be a graph. 
For any $S \subseteq V$, let $\partial S$ denote the set of neighbors of $S$ in $G$, i.e., $\partial S = \set{ v \in V \setminus S \mid \exists u \in S, \set{u, v} \in E }$; and let $G[S]$ denote the subgraph induced in $G$ by $S$, i.e., the graph with vertex set $S$ and edge set consisting of all edges of $G$ that have both endpoints in $S$.

Let $T=(V,E)$ be a tree rooted at $r$. 
For every vertex $v\in V$, let $T_v$ denote the subtree of $T$ rooted at $v$ that consists of all descendants of $v$; in particular, $T_r = T$.
For any $v\in V$, let $L(v)$ denote the set of children of $v$ in $T$.

We end this subsection by defining the ($t$-fold) convolution of distributions on $\Z$.
\begin{definition}[($t$-fold) Convolution]\label{def:t-fold-conv}
    Let $\mu,\nu$ be two distributions on $\Z$.
    Define a new distribution $\mu*\nu$ on $\Z$ by
    \begin{align*}
        \mu*\nu(k)=\sum_{i=-\infty}^{+\infty} \mu(i) \nu(k-i), \quad \forall k\in\Z.
    \end{align*}
    We call $\mu*\nu$ the \emph{convolution} of $\mu$ and $\nu$.
    Define $\mu^{*t}$ where $t\in \Z^+$ inductively by $\mu^{*1} = \mu$ and $\mu^{*t} = \mu^{*(t-1)} * \mu$ for $t \ge 2$.
    We call $\mu^{*t}$ the \emph{$t$-fold convolution} of $\mu$ with itself.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Spectral independence via coupling on trees}
\label{subsec:CI-to-SI}
The core result of this work is to establish the $O(\sqrt{n})$-spectral independence for the critical hardcore model, from which \cref{thm:main} readily follows by sophisticated spectral independence techniques that have been developed in a recent line of works.


The following notion of influences is needed to define the meaning of spectral independence. 
\begin{definition}[Influence, \cite{ALO20}]
    Let $\mu$ be a distribution over $\{0,1\}^n$.
    For any $i,j \in [n]$ such that $\Pr[\mu]{\sigma_i = 0} > 0$ and $\Pr[\mu]{\sigma_i = 1} > 0$, define the (pairwise) influence from $i$ to $j$ as
    \begin{align*}
        \Psi_\mu(i,j) := \Pr[\sigma\sim\mu]{\sigma_j = 1 \mid \sigma_i = 1} - \Pr[\sigma\sim\mu]{\sigma_j = 1 \mid \sigma_i = 0}.
    \end{align*}
\end{definition}

In the setting of the hardcore model, the influences describe the correlation between two vertices, represented as Bernoulli random variables indicating whether the vertices are occupied.
Roughly speaking, the influence of one vertex on the other represents the difference of the marginal distribution on the second vertex when flipping the first vertex from occupied to unoccupied.

\begin{theorem}[$O(\sqrt{n})$-Spectral independence of critical hardcore model]
\label{thm:SI-critical}
    Let $\alpha \ge 0$ be a constant. 
    Consider the hardcore model on an $n$-vertex graph $G=(V,E)$ of maximum degree $\Delta \ge 3$ with fugacity $\lambda \le (1+\frac{\alpha}{\sqrt{n}}) \lambda_c(\Delta)$. 
    Then, for any vertex $u \in V$, we have
    \begin{align*}
        \sum_{v \in V} \left| \Psi_{\mu_{G,\lambda}}(u,v) \right|
        \le C_0 \sqrt{n},
    \end{align*}
    where $C_0=C_0(\alpha) > 0$ is a constant depending only on $\alpha$.
\end{theorem}

\cref{thm:SI-critical} states that the hardcore model in the regime of interest satisfies $\ell_\infty$ spectral independence with constant $O(\sqrt{n})$ (see \cref{def:SI} and also \cite{FGYZ22}).
An analogous result for the Ising model was previously shown in \cite{CCYZ24}.

Many methods have been introduced to establish the spectral independence property for various families of distributions. 
Here we adopt the \emph{coupling independence} approach introduced in \cite{CZ23} and apply it on a related tree, known as the \emph{self-avoiding walk tree} \cite{Wei06}. The formal definition and construction of this tree are omitted in this paper as we only need its existence, and we refer interested readers to the works \cite{Wei06,CLV23}.

We are interested in coupling two hardcore models on this tree, where in one copy the root is fixed to be occupied while in the other it is fixed to be unoccupied.
As we shall see soon in \cref{prop:CI-to-SI}, controlling the number of discrepancies between these two copies under a simple coupling procedure enables us to deduce spectral independence.
To formally describe this coupling, we first need a few definitions.
Let $T=(V,E)$ be a tree rooted at $r$ of maximum degree at most $\Delta$.
Consider the hardcore model on $T$ with fugacity $\lambda > 0$.
For each vertex $v$, let $p_v$ denote the probability that $v$ is occupied in the hardcore model on the subtree $T_v$, i.e., $p_v := \Pr[\mu_{T_v,\lambda}]{\sigma_v = 1}$, where we recall that $T_v$ is the subtree of $T$ rooted at $v$ that consists of all descendants of $v$.

We now describe a natural vertex-by-vertex \emph{greedy coupling} for the hardcore model on $T$ when the spin at root $r$ is flipped.
\begin{itemize}
    \item Initialization: $X_r = 1$ and $Y_r = 0$;
    \item While there exists $v \in V$ whose parent $u$ has already been revealed in $X$ and $Y$:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item If $X_u = Y_u$, then couple the whole subtree $T_v$ perfectly, i.e., $X_{T_v} = Y_{T_v}$;
        \item If $X_u = 1$ and $Y_u = 0$, then set $X_v = 0$ and sample $Y_v \sim \mathrm{Ber}(p_v)$;
        \item If $X_u = 0$ and $Y_u = 1$, then sample $X_v \sim \mathrm{Ber}(p_v)$ and set $Y_v = 0$;
    \end{itemize}
    \item Return $(X,Y)$.
\end{itemize}
It is straightforward to check that when the greedy coupling ends, $X$ is an independent set distributed as $\Pr[\mu_{T,\lambda}]{\;\cdot \mid \sigma_r = 1}$ and $Y$ as $\Pr[\mu_{T,\lambda}]{\;\cdot \mid \sigma_r = 0}$.

\begin{proposition}[Coupling on trees implies spectral independence, {\cite[Lemma 39]{CF24}, \cite[Proposition 4.3]{CZ23}}] \label{prop:CI-to-SI}
    Consider the hardcore model on an $n$-vertex graph $G=(V,E)$ of maximum degree $\Delta \ge 3$ with fugacity $\lambda > 0$. 
    For any $u \in V$, there exists a tree $T = T_{\mathrm{SAW}}(G,u)$ rooted at $r$ with maximum degree at most $\Delta$, such that if $(X,Y) \sim\CC$ is the greedy coupling on $T$,  
    then it holds
    \begin{align*}
        \sum_{v \in V} \left| \Psi_{\mu_{G,\lambda}}(u,v) \right|
        \le \E[(X,Y)\sim\CC]{|X \oplus Y| \wedge n},
    \end{align*}
    where $X \oplus Y$ denotes the symmetric difference of two sets $X,Y$, and recall that $|X \oplus Y| \wedge n:=\min\set{|X \oplus Y|, n}$.
\end{proposition}


Hence, to establish \cref{thm:SI-critical}, it suffices to bound the expected number of disagreements in the greedy coupling for the hardcore model on trees when the spins at the root are distinct.


\subsection{Coupling on trees via site percolation}\label{subsec:sp-to-coupling}



From the greedy coupling procedure above, we observe a natural \emph{site percolation} on the tree $T$ describing the appearance of disagreements. Every vertex $v$ is \emph{open} with probability $p_v$ independently, representing the occurrence of a disagreement at $v$, that is, $X_v \neq Y_v$; otherwise, the vertex $v$ is \emph{closed}. 
The root $r$ is always open, i.e., $p_r = 1$, since $X_r \neq Y_r$.
Our goal is to bound the size of the \emph{open cluster} containing the root, consisting of all open vertices that are connected to the root via a path of open vertices.

We now introduce some notations for the site percolation model.
Let $T=(V,E)$ be a tree rooted at $r$.
For any $v\in V$, let $p_v$ be the probability that $v$ is open, and we call $p_v$ the \emph{occupation probability} of $v$.
For simplicity, we assume $p_r=1$.
Let $P=\set{p_v}_{v\in V}$ be the list of occupation probabilities for all vertices, and call it the \emph{occupation probability list} of the site percolation model.
Finally, for the site percolation on $T$ with occupation probability list $P$, let $N(T,P)$ denote the random variable representing the size of the open cluster containing the root. 

From the construction of the greedy coupling and the site percolation above, we see that $|X \oplus Y| \overset{d}{=} N(T,P)$, where $p_v = \Pr[\mu_{T_v,\lambda}]{\sigma_v = 1}$ for each $v\in V\setminus\set r$; see \cref{prop:SP-to-CI} for a formal statement.
Thus, the problem is reduced to studying a site percolation model.

In order to study this site percolation model, we need to know the conditions satisfied by the occupation probabilities $\{p_v\}$. 
Since these probabilities correspond to the marginal probability of the roots in the respective subtrees, they satisfy a well-known recurrence called the \emph{tree recursion} (see \cref{fact:tree-recursion}).
In this work, we present a new inequality satisfied by these marginal probabilities, which is crucial in obtaining our main spectral independence result.
In particular, \cref{eq:perc-prob-bd} below provides a stronger and simpler contraction property of the tree recursion, which was not known in the literature as far as we are aware.
For simplicity, here we consider only the exact critical fugacity $\lambda = \lambda_c(\Delta)$. 

\begin{lemma}[Special case of \cref{lem:contract-full}; see also \cite{LLY13}]\label{lem:contract}
    Let $T=(V,E)$ be a tree rooted at $r$ with maximum degree at most $\Delta$. 
    Consider the critical hardcore model on $T$ with fugacity $\lambda = \lambda_c(\Delta)$.
    For each vertex $v \in V$, let $p_v$ denote the probability that $v$ is occupied in the critical hardcore model on the subtree $T_v$ rooted at $v$, i.e., $p_v := \Pr[\mu_{T_v,\lambda}]{\sigma_v = 1}$.
    Then, for every non-root vertex $v \in V \setminus \{r\}$, it holds
    \begin{align}\label{eq:perc-prob-bd}
        p_{v} \sum_{w \in L(v)} p_w \le \frac{1}{\Delta-1},
    \end{align}
    where we recall that $L(v)$ denotes the set of children of $v$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{remark}
    In \cite{LLY13}, a potential function approach was applied to study the contraction of the tree recursion for the subcritical hardcore model. Uncovering their result (\cite[Lemma 12]{LLY13}), the corresponding condition they established at criticality can be stated as
    \begin{align}\label{eq:LLY}
        \sqrt{p_{v}} \sum_{w \in L(v)} \sqrt{p_w} \le 1.
    \end{align}
    Our bound \cref{eq:perc-prob-bd} is stronger than \cref{eq:LLY} since $|L(v)| \le \Delta-1$. 
    In fact, going through the proof in \cite{LLY13}, one is able to recover the stronger inequality \cref{eq:perc-prob-bd} though it was not stated explicitly.
    In this work, we provide a simpler proof of \cref{eq:perc-prob-bd} (and hence \cref{eq:LLY}).
    To establish the $O(\sqrt{n})$-spectral independence at criticality, we do require the stronger inequality \cref{eq:perc-prob-bd}, while the weaker \cref{eq:LLY} is not sufficient.
\end{remark}

We are now ready to state our main percolation result, from which spectral independence \cref{thm:SI-critical} readily follows.

\begin{theorem}[Main result for site percolation]\label{thm:percolation}
    Consider the site percolation model on the infinite $d$-ary tree $\T_{d}^{\-{ary}} = (V,E)$ rooted at $r$ with occupation probability list
    $P=\{p_v\}_{v \in V}$ where $p_r = 1$.
    Let $N=N(\T_d^{\-{ary}},P)$ be the size of the open cluster containing the root.
    Suppose the following hold:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item For every non-root vertex $v\in V \setminus \{r\}$, it holds 
        \begin{align}\label{eq:p_v-bound}
            p_{v} \sum_{w \in L(v)} p_w \le \frac{1}{d};
        \end{align}

        \item At the root $r$, it holds
        \begin{align}\label{eq:p_r-bound}
            \sum_{w \in L(r)} p_w \leq c,
        \end{align}
        where $c > 0$ is a constant.
    \end{enumerate}
    Then, we have that for any $n \in \Z_{\ge 0}$,
    \begin{align*}
        \E{N \wedge n} = O(\sqrt{n}),
    \end{align*}
    where the constant in big-O depends only on $c$.
\end{theorem}




At this point, we transfer our original problem of bounding the mixing time and proving critical spectral independence into a problem of site percolation on trees. 
In \cite{CCYZ24}, the same strategy was applied to the critical Ising model, another canonical example of graphical models. There, the occupation probabilities are much simpler; in fact, they can all be set to $p_v = 1/d$, which is a uniform upper bound on the pairwise influence on $v$ from its parent. 
We remark that the main distinction of \cite{CCYZ24} and our work lies in the application of \cref{lem:contract} (corresponding to the condition \cref{eq:p_v-bound}) which substantially extends the uniform setting, $p_v = 1/d$ for all $v$, appeared in the critical Ising model.


\subsection{Site percolation on trees via online decision making}\label{subsec:Hardcore-game}
The main part of the paper aims to prove \cref{thm:percolation}. 
We introduce a novel approach to study such a site percolation model on the infinite tree, by considering an online decision-making game.

Our strategy is to upper bound $\E{N \wedge n}$ by understanding the worst-case choice of occupation probabilities $\{p_v\}$.
We do this by changing the perspective and thinking as an adversary who is allowed to pick the occupation probabilities.
Namely, we study an online decision-making problem where a player is allowed to pick the occupation probabilities $\{p_v\}$ every time we need to reveal the status (open or closed) of a few vertices. These probabilities can be arbitrary as long as they satisfy the requirements \cref{eq:p_v-bound}, and the goal of the player is to maximize $\E{N \wedge n}$.

To be more specific, let $A_t$ denote the number of active vertices at time $t$, where a vertex is said to be \emph{active} if (i) it is open; (ii) there is an open path from it to the root; (iii) the status of its children has not been fully revealed.
At the beginning, $A_0 = 1$ since only the root is open and we have not revealed any other vertex.
Then, the player picks the occupation probabilities for both the children and the grandchildren of $r$; these probabilities are required to satisfy \cref{eq:p_v-bound}. 
By sampling from the corresponding Bernoulli distributions independently, we reveal the number of grandchildren of $r$, denoted as $X_1$, that are active (i.e., open and connected to $r$). 
With $r$ being deactivated, the number of active vertices becomes $A_1 = A_0 - 1 + X_1 = X_1$.
The game is then repeated. Whenever there exists an active vertex $v$ at round $t$, the player picks occupation probabilities of the children and grandchildren of $v$ satisfying \cref{eq:p_v-bound}, and, after the number $X_t$ of open grandchildren connected to $v$ is revealed, updates $A_t = A_{t-1} -1 + X_t$.
This process stops when there are no active vertices, i.e., when $A_t = 0$.

We remark that we consider only active vertices at even levels because \cref{eq:p_v-bound} imposes requirements on two adjacent levels.

Suppose the game stops after $k$ rounds. Then, the number of open vertices connected to the root at even levels is precisely $k$, since every such vertex becomes active at some point and is deactivated at some other time.
Therefore, controlling the number of rounds played in the game allows us to bound $\E{N_e \wedge n}$ where $N_e$ is the number of vertices at even levels that are in the open cluster containing the root in the site percolation. 
(Since $\E{N_e \wedge n} = \sum_{m = 1}^n \Pr{N_e \ge m}$, in the actual proof we aim to bound $\Pr{N_e \ge m}$ for every $m$ for simplicity.
And we can bound $\Pr{N_e \ge m}$ by the maximum probability that the game lasts for at least $m$ rounds.)
Finally, combining the upper bound of $\E{N_e \wedge n}$ with \cref{eq:p_r-bound}, it is then not hard to upper bound $\E{N \wedge n}$ as wanted.


Therefore, our goal is to determine the optimal strategy of the player when such an online decision-making game is played.
We deal with this in \cref{sec:game} where we state and prove our main result, \cref{thm:game-main}.
While a natural guess of the optimal strategy is to set every occupation probability $p_v$ to be $1/d$ so that \cref{eq:p_v-bound} is satisfied, we show that the optimal strategy of the player should set $p_v p_{w}=1/d$ for one child $w \in L(v)$ of $v$ and set $p_{w'} = 0$ for all other children $w' \in L(v)$.
We remark that such choices of $\{p_v\}$ are not realizable as marginal probabilities in the hardcore model since they do not satisfy the tree recursion and are way too large (in reality, $p_v = O(\lambda) = O(1/d)$ at criticality); however, they are sufficient to provide meaningful upper bounds on $\E{N \wedge n}$ as we need.



We present the proof of \cref{thm:percolation} about site percolation on the infinite tree in \cref{sec:percolation}, utilizing \cref{thm:game-main}.
Finally, we deduce spectral independence (\cref{thm:SI-critical}) and rapid mixing (\cref{thm:main}) from \cref{thm:percolation} in \cref{sec:proof-SI-mixing}.












\section{Online Decision-Making Problem}
\label{sec:game}
In this section, we introduce an online decision-making problem that serves as a key tool in proving our main result for site percolation (\cref{thm:percolation}), and show the optimal strategy as our main result for this section.

First of all, we describe the setup of the online decision-making game in \cref{subsec:game-setup}.
Then, we define a partial order called second-order stochastic dominance, which plays an important role in finding the optimal strategy, and show some basic properties in \cref{subsec:sosd}.
After that, we introduce the Poisson binomial distribution in \cref{subsec:PB} and some properties of the random walk hitting time in \cref{subsec:RWHT}, which are crucial in the proof of the optimal strategy.
Finally, in \cref{subsec:game-main}, we state and prove our main result for the online decision-making game and show the optimal strategy of the game.

\subsection{Setup of online decision making} \label{subsec:game-setup}
We now describe precisely an online decision-making game under a slightly more general setting. 

In the online decision-making game, the player maintains some number of tokens (corresponding to active vertices).
Let $A_t$ denote the number of tokens the player owns after round $t$.
At the beginning, the player has $A_0=a$ tokens.
There is a family $\PP$ of distributions on non-negative integers $\Z_{\ge 0}$ (corresponding to choices of occupation probabilities).
For round $t$, the player spends one token, assuming $A_{t-1} \ge 1$, and chooses a distribution $\pi_t \in \PP$. 
Then, a sample $X_t \sim \pi_t$ is generated independently, and the player receives $X_t$ tokens as a reward.
The number of tokens the player owns then becomes $A_t = A_{t-1}-1+X_t$. 
The game ends when the player uses up all the tokens, i.e., the first time $A_t = 0$.
We denote this stopping time by $\tau$.
Note that it is possible the game never stops, in which case $\tau=\infty$.
The goal of the player is to survive for $m$ rounds for some given integer $m \in \Z_{\geq0}$.
That is, the player wins if and only if $\tau \ge m$.
We present the process of the online decision-making game in \cref{alg:game}.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Online decision-making game}
\label{alg:game}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Input{A family $\PP$ of distributions on $\Z_{\ge 0}$, initial number of tokens $a \in \Z_{\geq0}$}
\Output{$t \leftarrow 0$, $A_0\leftarrow a$} 
\While{$A_t\geq1$}
\State $t \leftarrow t+1$;
\State Player chooses a distribution $\pi_t\in \PP$;
\State Sample $X_t \sim \pi_t$;
\State $A_t \leftarrow A_{t-1}-1+X_t$;
\EndWhile
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

Define a \emph{strategy} $\SS$ for the player by a mapping $\SS:\Z^+ \times \Z^+ \rightarrow \PP$. 
For any $k,a \in \Z^+$, $\SS(k,a)$ is defined by the distribution the player will choose when they needs to survive for $k$ more rounds to win and currently has $a$ tokens.
For example, if the winning requirement is $m$ rounds, then at the beginning of round $t$, the player will choose the distribution $\pi_t = \SS(m-t+1, A_{t-1})$ assuming $A_{t-1} \ge 1$.

For $m, a \in \Z_{\geq0}$, define the \emph{winning probability} under strategy $\SS$ to be
    \begin{align*}
        \varphi_{m}^\SS(a) := \tPr[\SS]{\tau \ge m \mid A_0 = a}. 
    \end{align*}
Namely, $\varphi_m^\SS(a)$ is the probability that the game lasts for at least $m$ rounds, assuming the player has $a$ tokens at the beginning and uses strategy $\SS$.
We further define
    \begin{align*}
        \varphi_{m}^*(a) := \sup_{\SS} \varphi_{m}^\SS(a).
    \end{align*}

The following lemma establishes a simple recursive formula for the optimal winning probabilities and the existence of an optimal strategy.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:game_recurance}
    Let $\PP$ be a family of distributions on $\Z_{\geq0}$.
    Suppose the metric space $(\PP,d_\mathrm{TV})$ is compact, where we recall $d_\mathrm{TV}$ is the total variation distance.
    Then the following holds:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item \label{item:lem:game_recurance-1}
        For all $m,a\in \Z^+$,
            \begin{align*}
            \varphi^*_m(a) 
            = \max_{\pi \in \PP} \; \E[X\sim\pi]{\varphi^*_{m-1}(a-1+X)};
            \end{align*}
        \item \label{item:lem:game_recurance-2}
        There exists a strategy $\SS^*: \Z^+ \times \Z^+ \rightarrow \PP$ such that $\varphi_{m}^*(a) = \varphi_{m}^{\SS^*}(a)$ holds for any $m,a\in \Z^+$.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
    We note that $\varphi_m^{\SS^*}(a)$ is well-defined if $\SS^*(k,\cdot)$ is defined for all $1\leq k\leq m$, and the result of $\SS^*(k,\cdot)\ (k>m)$ does not matter. 
    In our proof, when we write $\varphi_m^{\SS^*}(a)$, we only guarantee that for all $1\leq k\leq m$, $\SS^*(k,\cdot)$ is defined. 

    We verify the recurrence and define the strategy inductively on $m$.

    For the base case $m=1$, by definition, $\varphi^*_1(a)=\one[a\geq1]$, and $\varphi^*_0(a)=1$ for all $a\in \Z_{\geq 0}$.
    Then \cref{item:lem:game_recurance-1} immediately follows.
    Furthermore, we can define $\SS^*(1,\cdot) :=\pi_0$ so that $\varphi^*_{1}(a) = \varphi_{1}^{\SS^*}(a)$ holds for any $a\in \Z^+$, where $\pi_0\in \PP$ is an arbitrary distribution.

    Now suppose $m \ge 2$. 
    Suppose \cref{item:lem:game_recurance-1,item:lem:game_recurance-2} hold for $m-1$.
    Let $a\in \Z^+$. Suppose the player chooses $\pi \in \PP$ in the first round and obtains $X \sim \pi$ tokens; hence, after the first round, the player has $A_1 = A_0-1+X = a-1+X$ tokens. 
    Then to maximize the winning probability, i.e., to maximize the probability that survive for at least $m-1$ rounds when having $a-1+X$ tokens initally, the player should use strategy $\SS^*(k,\cdot)$ ($k=1,\cdots,m-1$), and then the winning probability is $\varphi^*_{m-1}(a-1+X)$ by the induction hypothesis $\varphi_{m-1}^*(a-1+X)=\varphi_{m-1}^{\SS^*}(a-1+X)$.
    Therefore, if the player chooses $\pi \in \PP$ in the first round, the maximum winning probability for the player is $\E[X\sim\pi]{\varphi^*_{m-1}(a-1+X)}$.
    Hence, to obtain the maximum winning probability, we need to choose an optimal $\pi$ which maximizes $\E[X\sim\pi]{\varphi^*_{m-1}(a-1+X)}$. 
    By compactness, such optimal $\pi$ exists, therefore \cref{item:lem:game_recurance-1} for $m$ holds, and we can define $\SS^*(m,\cdot)$ by $\SS^*(m,a) := \argmax_{\pi\in\PP} \E[X\sim\pi]{\varphi^*_{m-1}(a-1+X)}$ for all $a\in \Z^+$.
    Then it is straightforward that $\varphi_{m}^*(a) = \varphi_{m}^{\SS^*}(a)$ holds for any $a\in \Z^+$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Second-order stochastic dominance} \label{subsec:sosd}

Our goal is to find an optimal strategy for the online decision-making game.
A first thought that one may consider is to use \emph{first-order stochastic dominance} (also simply called \emph{stochastic dominance}). For any two distributions $\mu, \nu$ over $\Z_{\geq0}$, $\mu$ is (first-order) stochastically dominated by $\nu$ if and only if $\Pr[\mu]{X \geq i}\leq \Pr[\nu]{Y \geq i}$ for all $i\in \Z_{\geq0}$.
An immediate corollary is that $\mu$ is stochastically dominated by $\nu$ implies $\E[\mu]{X}\leq \E[\nu]{Y}$.
If there is a largest distribution under stochastic dominance in $\PP$, then it can be easily proved that the player can attain the maximum winning probability when always picking the largest distribution. 
However, the largest distribution under stochastic dominance does not exist in our case, since any two different distributions with the same mean are not comparable under stochastic dominance.
In fact, in our application, there are infinitely many distributions attaining the largest mean in $\PP$. Therefore, there is no largest distribution under stochastic dominance and we need something else.

It turns out that \emph{second-order stochastic dominance} (see, e.g., \cite{Lev92,GHW24} for more background and applications) can address the problem of the lack of a largest distribution.
However, as a trade-off, it is not always true that the player can achieve the maximum winning probability when always choosing the largest distribution under second-order stochastic dominance.
Nonetheless, if the largest distribution satisfies some nice properties (see \cref{thm:game-main} for details), this is indeed true. 

We now define second-order stochastic dominance.

\begin{definition}[Second-order stochastic dominance]
    We define a partial order ``$\oleq$'' called \emph{second-order stochastic dominance} on the family of distributions on $\Z_{\ge 0}$ with finite expectations. 
    For two distributions $\mu, \nu$ on $\Z_{\ge 0}$ with finite expectations,  
    $\mu \oleq \nu$ if and only if 
    \begin{align*}
        \E[X \sim \mu]{X \wedge i} \leq \E[Y \sim \nu]
        {Y \wedge i}, \quad \forall i \in \Z^+.
    \end{align*}
\end{definition}

The following proposition shows some classical equivalent definitions of second-order stochastic dominance.

\begin{proposition}[Equivalent definitions of second-order stochastic dominance {\cite[Theorem 8.1.1]{MS02}, see also \cite{RS70}}] 
\label{prop:sosd}

    Let $\mu,\nu$ be distributions on $\Z_{\ge 0}$ with finite expectations.
    The following definitions are equivalent:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item \label{item:prop:sosd-1} $\mu \oleq \nu$; 
        \item \label{item:prop:sosd-2} (Increasing concave order) For any non-decreasing concave function $f$, it holds: 
        \begin{align*}
            \E[X\sim\mu]{f(X)} \le \E[X\sim\nu]{f(X)};
        \end{align*}
        \item \label{item:prop:sosd-3} 
            There exists a coupling $\CC$ of $\mu$ and $\nu$ such that
            \begin{align*}
                \E[(X,Y)\sim\CC]{X-Y \mid Y=i} \leq 0, \quad \forall i\in\Z_{\geq0}.
            \end{align*}
    \end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

The following two lemmas offer easy ways to find an ``upper bound'' of a given distribution in the sense of ``$\oleq$'', and are helpful to us in identifying the largest distribution in $\PP$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:sd-ber}
    Let $\mu$ be a distribution on $\Z_{\ge 0}$
    with expectation $\E[\mu]{X} \le \gamma \le 1$.
    Then $\mu \oleq \mathrm{Ber}(\gamma)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
    For any $i\in \Z^+$, we have 
    \begin{align*}
        \E[X \sim \mu]{X \wedge i} \leq \E[X \sim \mu]{X}\leq\gamma=\E[Y \sim \mathrm{Ber}(\gamma)]
        {Y \wedge i},
    \end{align*}
    which implies $\mu \oleq \mathrm{Ber}(\gamma)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:sd-conv}
    If $\mu_1 \oleq \nu_1$ and $\mu_2 \oleq \nu_2$, then $\mu_1 * \mu_2 \oleq \nu_1 * \nu_2$, where the operator ``*'' represents convolution \textnormal{(see \cref{def:t-fold-conv})}. 
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
    Let $X_1,X_2$ be independent random variables with distributions $\mu_1,\mu_2$ respectively.
    Let $Y_1,Y_2$ be independent random variables with distributions $\nu_1,\nu_2$ respectively.
    We also assume $X_1,Y_2$ are independent and $X_2,Y_1$ are independent.
    For $i=1,2$, by $\mu_i \oleq \nu_i$ and \cref{prop:sosd}, there exists a coupling $\CC_i$ of $(X_i,Y_i)$, such that 
    \begin{align} \label{eq:sosd>=0}
        \E[(X_i,Y_i)\sim\CC_i]{X_i-Y_i \mid Y_i=j} \leq 0, \quad \forall j\in\Z_{\geq0}.
    \end{align}
    Let $X=X_1+X_2, Y=Y_1+Y_2$.
    Then $\mu_1*\mu_2$ is the law of $X$, and $\nu_1*\nu_2$ is the law of $Y$. 
    Let $\CC$ be the joint distribution of $(X,Y)$ assuming $(X_i,Y_i)\sim \CC_i\ (i=1,2)$.
    It is clear that $\CC$ is a coupling of $\mu_1*\mu_2$ and $\nu_1*\nu_2$.
    For any $k\in \Z_{\geq0}$,
    \begin{align*}
        \E[(X,Y)\sim \CC]{X-Y\vert Y=k} 
        &= \sum_{i=1}^2\E[\substack{(X_1,Y_1)\sim \CC_1,\\(X_2,Y_2)\sim \CC_2}]{X_i-Y_i\vert Y=k}\\
        &= \sum_{i=1}^2 \sum_{j=0}^{k} \Pr[\substack{(X_1,Y_1)\sim \CC_1,\\(X_2,Y_2)\sim \CC_2}]{Y_i=j|Y=k} \E[(X_i,Y_i)\sim \CC_i]{X_i-Y_i\vert Y_i=j}\\
        &\leq 0,
    \end{align*}
    where the last inequality follows from \cref{eq:sosd>=0}.
    Then \cref{lem:sd-conv} follows from \cref{prop:sosd}.
\end{proof}


\subsection{Poisson binomial distribution}
\label{subsec:PB}

As hinted by \cref{item:prop:sosd-2} of \cref{prop:sosd}, in order to apply second-order stochastic dominance, we hope to have some non-decreasing concave functions as the objective/utility function in our decision making.
It turns out that when the largest distribution (under ``$\oleq$'') is a \emph{Poisson binomial distribution} (see, e.g., \cite{TT23} for a thorough introduction) with expectation at least $1$, the maximum winning probability $\varphi^*_{m}(a)$ is non-decreasing and concave with respect to $a$. 

We first define the Poisson binomial distribution.

\begin{definition}[Poisson binomial distributions (random variables)]
    We call a random variable $X$ a Poisson binomial random variable if it can be expressed as a finite sum of independent Bernoulli random variables, i.e., $X = \sum_{i=1}^\ell X_i$ where $\ell \in \Z^+$, $X_i \sim \-{Ber}(p_i)$ are independent.
    We call a distribution a Poisson binomial distribution if it is the distribution of a Poisson binomial random variable.
\end{definition}

An immediate property is the following.
\begin{fact}\label{fact:PB}
    If $X$ and $Y$ are two independent Poisson binomial random variables, then $X+Y$ is also a Poisson binomial random variable.
\end{fact}

The crucial property that guarantees the concavity of the maximum winning probability is that a Poisson binomial random variable is unimodal with a mode near the mean of the random variable.
We next define unimodality and state the property.

\begin{definition}[Unimodality \cite{DJd88}]\label{def:unimode}
    Let $\pi$ be a distribution on $\Z$.
    Let $m$ be an integer.
    The distribution $\pi$ is called \emph{unimodal} about $m$
    if
    \begin{align*}
        \pi(i)\geq \pi(i-1), \quad \forall i\leq m 
        \quad \-{and}\quad
        \pi(i)\geq \pi(i+1), \quad \forall i\geq m,
    \end{align*}
    and we call $m$ a \emph{mode} of $\pi$.

    Let $Z$ be a random variable with distribution $\pi$.
    The random variable $Z$ is called unimodal about $m$ if $\pi$ is unimodal about $m$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Darroch's rule for the mode \cite{Dar64}]\label{thm:mode}
    Let $Z$ be a Poisson binomial random variable.
    Let $q$ be the expectation of $Z$.
    Then there exists $m\in\set{\floor{q},\floor{q}+1}$ if $q \notin \Z$, or $m=q$ if $q \in \Z$, such that $Z$ is unimodal about $m$.
    In particular, 
    \begin{align*}
        \Pr{Z=i} \geq \Pr{Z=i-1}, \quad \forall i\leq q 
        \quad \-{and}\quad
        \Pr{Z=i} \geq \Pr{Z=i+1}, \quad \forall i\geq q.
    \end{align*}
\end{proposition}

\subsection{Random walk hitting time} \label{subsec:RWHT}

    The following proposition of the random walk hitting time will be used in deriving the formula of the maximum winning probability and proving the concavity of the maximum winning probability.

    \begin{proposition}
    \label{prop:hitting-time}
        Let $\{W_t\}_{t= 0}^{\infty}$ be a random walk on $\=Z$. Specifically, $W_t = \sum_{i=1}^t Y_i$, where $Y_i$ are independent and identically distributed integer-valued random variables satisfying $\Pr{Y_1 \geq - 1} = 1$ (left-continuous).
        For any $a\in\Z^+$, define the hitting time $\tau_{-a} := \min\set{t \geq 0 \mid W_t = -a}$, with the convention that $\tau_{-a}:=\infty$ if $W_t \neq -a$ for all $t\geq0$.
        Then the following holds:
        \begin{enumerate}
            \item\label{item:prop:hitting-time-1} \textnormal{(\cite{vdHK08})} For every $m, a \in \Z^{+}$, 
            \begin{align*}
                \Pr{\tau_{-a} = m} = \frac{a}{m} \Pr{W_m = -a};
            \end{align*}

            \item\label{item:prop:hitting-time-2} For any $a\in\Z^+$,  
            \begin{align*}
                \Pr{\tau_{-a} =\infty} 
                = 1-\tp{1- \Pr{\tau_{-1} =\infty}}^a
                \leq a\Pr{\tau_{-1} =\infty}.
        \end{align*}
        \end{enumerate}
    \end{proposition}
    We refer the readers to \cite{vdHK08} for the proof of \cref{item:prop:hitting-time-1}, and we defer the proof of \cref{item:prop:hitting-time-2} to \cref{app:missing proofs-no-stop}.

\subsection{Determining optimal strategy}\label{subsec:game-main}

In this subsection, we show our main result for the online decision-making game. 
The following theorem implies that if the largest distribution $\pi^*$ (under ``$\oleq$'') of $\PP$ exists and is a Poisson binomial distribution with expectation at least $1$, then an optimal strategy $\SS^*$ for the player is $\SS^*\equiv \pi^*$, in other words, the player can achieve the maximum winning probability when always choosing the largest distribution. 
\begin{theorem}[Main result for online decision making]\label{thm:game-main}
    Let $\PP$ be a family of distributions on $\Z_{\geq0}$.
    Suppose the metric space $(\PP,d_\mathrm{TV})$ is compact.
    Suppose there exists a largest distribution in $\PP$ under the partial order ``$\oleq$'', denoted by $\pi^*$.
    Furthermore, suppose $\pi^*$ is a Poisson binomial distribution with expectation at least $1$.
    Then the following holds:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item \label{item:game-main-1} (Recurrence) For any $m,a \in \Z^+$,
        \begin{align*}
            \varphi^*_m(a) = \E[X\sim\pi^*]{\varphi^*_{m-1}(a-1+X)};
        \end{align*}
        Namely, the player will pick $\pi^*$ to achieve the maximum winning probability, and the optimal strategy is $\SS^*(m,a) = \pi^*$ for all $m,a \in \Z^+$.

        \item \label{item:game-main-2} (Formula) Let $Z_1,Z_2,\cdots$ be independent and identically distributed random variables with distribution $\pi^*$,
        and let $S_t=\sum_{i=1}^t (Z_i-1)$.
        For any $a\in\Z^+$, define $\tau_{-a}:=\min\{t\geq0 \mid S_t=-a\}$, with the convention that $\tau_{-a}:=\infty$ if $S_t \neq -a$ for all $t\geq0$.
        For any $m,a \in \Z^+$, it holds that
        \begin{align*}
            \varphi^*_m(a) =\Pr{\tau_{-a}\geq m}= \sum_{t \ge m} \frac{a}{t} \Pr{S_t = -a}+\Pr{\tau_{-a}=\infty};
        \end{align*}
        \item \label{item:game-main-3} (Concavity) For any $m \in \Z^+$, the function $\varphi^*_m$ is concave:
        \begin{align*}
            2\varphi^*_m(a) \ge \varphi^*_m(a-1) + \varphi^*_m(a+1), \quad \forall a \in \Z^+.
        \end{align*}
    \end{enumerate}     
\end{theorem}

We will prove by induction on $m$. 
The recurrence follows from the concavity of the maximum winning probability and \cref{prop:sosd} about second-order stochastic dominance. 
Given the recurrence, we obtain the formula using \cref{prop:hitting-time} about the random walk hitting time. 
Finally, we derive concavity using the formula and the fact that $\pi^*$ is a Poisson binomial distribution and hence satisfies unimodality with mode near the mean.

\begin{proof}
    We prove by induction on $m$.

\medskip
    \noindent \emph{Base case:} $m=1$. By definition, $\varphi^*_1(a)=\one[a\geq 1]$. It is easy to check that \cref{item:game-main-1,item:game-main-3} hold for $m=1$. 
    For \cref{item:game-main-2}, the first equality holds trivially for $m=1$, the proof for the second equality for $m=1$ is the same as that for arbitrary $m \geq 2$, i.e., applying \cref{item:prop:hitting-time-1} of \cref{prop:hitting-time}, which we will show in the inductive step below.

\medskip
\noindent \emph{Inductive step:}
    For any $m \ge 2$, suppose \cref{item:game-main-1,item:game-main-2,item:game-main-3} hold for $m-1$, and we aim to prove \cref{item:game-main-1,item:game-main-2,item:game-main-3} hold for $m$.

\medskip   
    \noindent \emph{1. Recurrence for $m$} 

    By \cref{lem:game_recurance}, it holds that
    \begin{align}\label{eq:game_rec}
        \varphi^*_m(a) = \max_{\pi \in \PP} \; \E[X\sim\pi]{\varphi^*_{m-1}(a-1+X)}.
    \end{align}
    By definition, it is clear that $\varphi^*_{m-1}(a-1+X)$ is a non-decreasing function with respect to $X$.
    By Concavity for $m-1$,
    $\varphi^*_{m-1}(a-1+X)$ is a concave function with respect to $X$.
    By assumption, $\pi \oleq \pi^*$ holds for any $\pi\in \PP$.
    For any $\pi\in \PP$, applying the equivalence between \cref{item:prop:sosd-1} and \cref{item:prop:sosd-2} of \cref{prop:sosd} with $\mu=\pi,\nu=\pi^*$ and $f(X)=\varphi^*_{m-1}(a-1+X)$, it holds that
    \begin{align} \label{eq:sosd}
        \E[X\sim\pi]{\varphi^*_{m-1}(a-1+X)}
        \leq \E[X\sim\pi^*]{\varphi^*_{m-1}(a-1+X)}.
    \end{align}
    \cref{eq:game_rec} and \cref{eq:sosd} imply Recurrence for $m$.

\medskip   
    \noindent \emph{2. Formula for $m$}

    We first explain why $\varphi^*_m(a) =\Pr{\tau_{-a}\geq m}$ is true intuitively.
    Recall that $Z_1,Z_2,\cdots$ are independent and identically distributed random variables with distribution $\pi^*$, representing the player choosing the largest distribution $\pi^*$ every time.
    Then $S_t=\sum_{i=1}^t (Z_i-1)$ represents the net income of tokens after round $t$ when the player chooses the largest distribution $\pi^*$ every time.
    Then, $\tau_{-a}$ is the first time that the net income of tokens is $-a$, i.e., the time the game stops if the player initially gets $a$ tokens.
    Therefore, $\Pr{\tau_{-a} \geq m}$ is the probability that the game lasts for at least $m$ rounds when the player chooses the largest distribution $\pi^*$ every time.
    Then $\varphi^*_m(a) =\Pr{\tau_{-a}\geq m}$ follows from the fact that maximum winning probability can be obtained from choosing $\pi^*$ every time, where the fact follows from Recurrence for $1,2,\cdots, m$.

    We next prove $\varphi^*_m(a) =\Pr{\tau_{-a}\geq m}$ formally from the induction hypothesis.
    For any $a\in\Z^{+}$, we have that
    \begin{align*}
        \varphi^*_{m}(a)
        &=\E[X\sim\pi^*]{\varphi^*_{m-1}(a-1+X)}\tag{Recurrence for $m$}\\
        &=\sum_{i=0}^\infty \pi^*(i)\Pr{\tau_{-(a-1+i)}\geq m-1}\tag{Formula for $m-1$}\\
        &=\sum_{i=0}^\infty \Pr{Z_1=i}\Pr{\tau_{-a}\geq m\mid Z_1=i}\nonumber\\
        &=\Pr{\tau_{-a}\geq m}, 
    \end{align*}
    as desired.

    For the the second equality of Formula for $m$, we apply \cref{item:prop:hitting-time-1} of \cref{prop:hitting-time} with $Y_t=Z_t-1$ and $W_t=\sum_{i=1}^t Y_i=\sum_{i=1}^t (Z_i-1)=S_t$. Then it follows
    \begin{align*}
        \Pr{\tau_{-a}\geq m}&=\sum_{t = m}^{\infty}\Pr{\tau_{-a} = t} + \Pr{\tau_{-a} =\infty}\\
        &= \sum_{t = m}^{\infty} \frac{a}{t} \Pr{S_t = -a} + \Pr{\tau_{-a} =\infty},
    \end{align*}
    as desired.

\medskip   
    \noindent \emph{3. Concavity for $m$}

    For any $a \geq 2$, it holds that
    \begin{align*}
        2 \varphi^*_m(a) 
        &= \E[X\sim\pi^*]{2 \varphi^*_{m-1}(a-1+X)} \tag{Recurrence for $m$}\\
        &\geq \E[X\sim\pi^*]{\varphi^*_{m-1}(a-2+X) + \varphi^*_{m-1}(a+X)} \tag{Concavity for $m-1$} \\
        &= \E[X\sim\pi^*]{\varphi^*_{m-1}(a-2+X)} + \E[X\sim\pi^*]{\varphi^*_{m-1}(a+X)} \\
        &= \varphi^*_m(a-1) + \varphi^*_m(a+1). \tag{Recurrence for $m$}
    \end{align*}
    It remains to prove the case $a=1$, i.e.,
    \begin{align*}
        2 \varphi^*_m(1) \ge \varphi^*_m(2).
    \end{align*}
    By Formula for $m$, 
    \begin{align*}
        \varphi^*_m(1)
        &=\sum_{t = m}^{\infty} \frac{1}{t} \Pr{S_t = -1} + \Pr{\tau_{-1} =\infty}\\
        &=\sum_{t = m}^{\infty} \frac{1}{t} \Pr{\sum_{i=1}^t Z_i = t-1} + \Pr{\tau_{-1} =\infty},
    \end{align*}
    \begin{align*}
        \varphi^*_m(2)=\sum_{t = m}^{\infty} \frac{2}{t} \Pr{\sum_{i=1}^t Z_i = t-2} + \Pr{\tau_{-2} =\infty}.
    \end{align*}
    It suffices to prove 
    \begin{align} \label{eq:unimode}
        \Pr{\sum_{i=1}^t Z_i = t-1} \geq \Pr{\sum_{i=1}^t Z_i = t-2},\quad \forall t\in\Z^+,
    \end{align}
    and
    \begin{align} \label{eq:inf-12}
        2\Pr{\tau_{-1} =\infty} \geq \Pr{\tau_{-2} =\infty}.
    \end{align}

    For any $t\in\Z^+$, since $\pi^*$ is a Poisson binomial distribution, by \cref{fact:PB}, $\sum_{i=1}^tZ_i$ is a Poisson binomial random variable.
    By \cref{thm:mode}, we have 
    \begin{align*}
        \Pr{\sum_{i=1}^t Z_i = j} \geq \Pr{\sum_{i=1}^t Z_i = j-1}
    \end{align*}
    for any $j\leq q$, where $q=\E{\sum_{i=1}^tZ_i}=t\E{Z_1}\geq t$, which implies \cref{eq:unimode}.    

    Applying \cref{item:prop:hitting-time-2} of \cref{prop:hitting-time} with $a=2$ yields \cref{eq:inf-12}.
    \end{proof}

\section{Site Percolation on Infinite Tree}
\label{sec:percolation}
In this section, we aim to prove the main result for the site percolation model \cref{thm:percolation} and show that it can be applied to the critical hardcore model.

In \cref{subsec:percolation-probability-bound}, we prove \cref{lem:contract} about a contraction property of the tree recursion of the hardcore model, which indicates that the main result for the site percolation model \cref{thm:percolation} can be applied to the critical hardcore model.
In \cref{subsec:site-percolation-main}, we prove the main result for the site percolation model \cref{thm:percolation} by applying the online decision-making game introduced in \cref{sec:game}.

\subsection{Contraction of tree recursion: Proof of \texorpdfstring{\cref{lem:contract}}{Lemma 2.5}}
\label{subsec:percolation-probability-bound}

In this subsection, we prove a general version of \cref{lem:contract}.
In the general version, we extend the domain of the fugacity $\lambda$ from at most the critical fugacity to at most $(1+\eps)$ times the critical fugacity.
And the original version (\cref{lem:contract}) can be obtained simply by letting $\eps=0$.

\begin{lemma}[General version of \cref{lem:contract}]\label{lem:contract-full}
    Let $T=(V,E)$ be a tree rooted at $r$ with maximum degree at most $\Delta$. 
    Let $\eps \ge 0$ be a constant.
    Consider the hardcore model on $T$ with fugacity $\lambda \le (1+\eps) \lambda_c(\Delta)$.
    For each vertex $v \in V$, let $p_v$ denote the probability that $v$ is occupied in the hardcore model with fugacity $\lambda$ on the subtree $T_v$ rooted at $v$, i.e., $p_v := \Pr[\mu_{T_v,\lambda}]{\sigma_v = 1}$.
    Then, for every non-root vertex $v \in V \setminus \{r\}$, it holds
    \begin{align*}
        p_{v} \sum_{w \in L(v)} p_w \le \frac{1+\e \eps}{\Delta-1}.
    \end{align*}
\end{lemma}

The following well-known fact gives a natural recurrence of the probability of the root being occupied in the hardcore model of subtrees.

\begin{fact}[Tree recursion, \cite{Wei06}]
\label{fact:tree-recursion}
    For $\{p_v\}$ defined in \cref{lem:contract-full}, and for any $v\in V$, it holds that
    \begin{align*}
        \frac{p_v}{1-p_v} = \lambda \prod_{w\in L(v)} (1-p_w).
    \end{align*}
\end{fact}

\begin{proof}[Proof of \cref{lem:contract-full}]
Fix $v \in V \setminus \{r\}$.
Let $d=\Delta-1$.
Then $v$ has at most $d$ children, i.e., $|L(v)|\leq d$.

By tree recursion \cref{fact:tree-recursion}, we have
\begin{align*}
    \frac{p_v}{1-p_v} = \lambda \prod_{w\in L(v)} (1-p_w)\leq \lambda \tp{1-\frac{1}{d}\sum_{w\in L(v)}p_w}^d = \lambda (1-\bar p)^d,
\end{align*}
where $\bar p = \frac{1}{d}\sum_{w\in L(v)}p_w$, and the inequality follows from the AM-GM inequality and $|L(v)|\leq d$.
Therefore, 
\begin{align*}
    p_v\sum_{w\in L(v)}p_w
    \leq\frac{\lambda (1-\bar p)^d}{1+\lambda (1-\bar p)^d}\sum_{w\in L(v)}p_w
    =d\bar p\frac{\lambda (1-\bar p)^d}{1+\lambda (1-\bar p)^d}.
\end{align*}
Set
\begin{align*}
    f(x)=x\frac{\lambda (1-x)^d}{1+\lambda (1-x)^d}, x \in[0,1].
\end{align*}
Since $\bar p\in[0,1]$, we have $p_v\sum_{w\in L(v)}p_w\leq d\max_{x\in[0,1]} f(x)$.
We next bound $\max_{x\in[0,1]} f(x)$.
By standard calculus calculation, we have 
\begin{align*}
    f'(x) = \frac{\lambda(1-x)^{d-1}}{\tp{1+\lambda(1-x)^d}^2}(\lambda(1-x)^{d+1}+(1+d)(1-x)-d).
\end{align*}
Set $g(x)=\lambda(1-x)^{d+1}+(1+d)(1-x)-d$.
Since the first factor of $f'(x)$ is always non-negative, the sign of $f'(x)$ depends on the second factor, i.e., $g(x)$.
Clearly, $g(x)$ is decreasing on $[0,1]$, $g(0)>0$, $g(1)<0$, by the Intermediate Value Theorem, there exists a unique zero of $g(x)$ on $[0,1]$, denoted by $\hat x$.
Then, 
\begin{align*}
    \max_{x\in[0,1]} f(x)
    &=f(\hat x)=\hat x\tp{1-\frac{1}{1+\lambda(1-\hat x)^d}}\\
    &=\hat x \tp{1-\frac{1}{1+\frac{d}{1-\hat x}-(1+d)}} \tag{by $g(\hat x)=0$}\\
    &= \hat x+\frac{\hat x-1}{d}.
\end{align*}
Therefore, 
\begin{align*}
    p_v\sum_{w\in L(v)}p_w\leq d\max_{x\in[0,1]} f(x)=(d+1)\hat x-1.
\end{align*}
When $\eps=0$, $\lambda\leq\lambda_c(\Delta)$, it holds that 
\begin{align*}
    g\tp{\frac{1}{d}} \leq \lambda_c(\Delta)\tp{1-\frac{1}{d}}^{d+1}+(1+d)\tp{1-\frac{1}{d}}-d=0.
\end{align*}
By $g(x)$ is decreasing on $[0,1]$, we have $\hat{x} \leq \frac{1}{d}$.
Then, 
\begin{align*}
    p_v\sum_{w\in L(v)}p_w \leq (d+1)\hat x-1 \leq \frac{1}{d},
\end{align*}
as desired.

We next prove for $\eps> 0$. 
Consider $f(x)=x\frac{\lambda (1-x)^d}{1+\lambda (1-x)^d}$ as a function with respect to both $x$ and $\lambda$. Let $h(\lambda,x)=f(x)=x\frac{\lambda (1-x)^d}{1+\lambda (1-x)^d}$. Then, by the result of the case $\eps=0$, we have $h(\lambda,x)\leq \frac{1}{d^2}$ for all $\lambda \leq \lambda_c(\Delta),x\in [0,1]$.
For all $\lambda>0,\ x\in[0,1]$, 
\begin{align*}
    h'_\lambda(\lambda,x)
    &=\frac{x(1-x)^d}{(1+\lambda(1-x)^{d})^2}\leq x(1-x)^d
    \leq \frac{1}{d} \tp{\frac{\tp{dx+d\tp{1-x}}}{d+1}}^{d+1}=\frac{1}{d}\tp{\frac{d}{d+1}}^{d+1},
\end{align*}
where the last inequality follows from the AM-GM inequality.
Then, by the Mean Value Theorem, for any $\lambda \leq (1+\eps) \lambda_c(\Delta), x\in [0,1]$, it holds that
\begin{align*}
    h(\lambda,x)
    &=h(\lambda_c(\Delta),x)+h'_\lambda(\lambda^*,x)(\lambda-\lambda_c(\Delta))\leq \frac{1}{d^2}+\frac{1}{d}\tp{\frac{d}{d+1}}^{d+1}\eps\lambda_c(\Delta)\\
    &=\frac{1}{d^2}\tp{1+\eps\tp{1+\frac{1}{d^2-1}}^{d+1}}
    \leq \frac{1}{d^2} \tp{1+\eps \e^{\frac{1}{d-1}}}
    \leq \frac{1+\e \eps}{d^2},
\end{align*}
where $\lambda^*$ is a real number between $\lambda_c(\Delta)$ and $\lambda$, and the second inequality follows from $1+x \leq \e^x$. 
Therefore, when $\lambda \leq (1+\eps) \lambda_c(\Delta)$, $f(x) \leq \frac{1+\e \eps}{d^2}$ holds for any $x\in [0,1]$. 
Then, 
\begin{align*}
    p_v\sum_{w\in L(v)}p_w \leq d\max_{x\in[0,1]} f(x)\leq \frac{1+\e \eps}{d},
\end{align*}
as desired.
\end{proof}


\subsection{Site percolation on trees: Proof of \texorpdfstring{\cref{thm:percolation}}{Theorem 2.7}}
\label{subsec:site-percolation-main}

In this subsection, we prove a general version of \cref{thm:percolation}.
In the general version, we extend the upper bound of $p_{v} \sum_{w \in L(v)} p_w$ from $\frac{1}{d}$ to $\frac{1}{d}(1+\frac{c_1}{\sqrt{n}})$. 
We note the latter upper bound can be derived from \cref{lem:contract-full}.
The original version (\cref{thm:percolation}) can be obtained simply by letting $c_1=0,c_2=c$.

\begin{theorem}[General version of \cref{thm:percolation}]\label{thm:percolation-full}
    Consider a site percolation model on the infinite $d$-ary tree $\T_{d}^{\-{ary}} = (V,E)$ rooted at $r$ with occupation probability list
    $P=\{p_v\}_{v \in V}$ where $p_r=1$.
    Let $N=N(\T_d^{\-{ary}}, P)$ be the size of the open cluster containing the root.
    Let $n \in \Z^+$ be a positive integer.
    Suppose the following conditions hold:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item \label{item:thm:percolation-full-1}
        For every non-root vertex $v\in V \setminus \{r\}$, it holds
        \begin{align*}
            p_{v} \sum_{w \in L(v)} p_w \le \frac{1}{d}\tp{1+\frac{c_1}{\sqrt n}},
        \end{align*}
        where $c_1 \ge 0$ is a constant;

        \item \label{item:thm:percolation-full-2}
        At the root $r$, it holds
        \begin{align}\label{eq:root_children_prob}
            \sum_{w \in L(r)} p_w \leq c_2,
        \end{align}
        where $c_2 > 0$ is a constant.
    \end{enumerate}
    Then, it holds that
    \begin{align*}
        \E{N \wedge n} = O(\sqrt{n}),
    \end{align*}
    where the constant in big-O depends only on $c_1,c_2$. 
\end{theorem}

We first show an upper bound with respect to $N_e$, the number of vertices at even levels that are in the open cluster containing the root, and it is straightforward to prove the upper bound with respect to $N$ when combining \cref{eq:root_children_prob}. 

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:percolation-even}
    Under the setting of \cref{thm:percolation-full}, let $N_e$ be the number of vertices at even levels that are in the open cluster containing the root.
    Then, we have 
    \begin{align*}
        \E{N_e \wedge n} = O(\sqrt{n}),
    \end{align*}
    where the constant in big-O depends only on $c_1$.
\end{lemma}



\begin{proof}[Proof of \cref{thm:percolation-full}]
Let $r_1,\cdots,r_d$ be $d$ children of $r$.
Recall that $N_e$ is the number of vertices at even levels that are in the open cluster containing the root.
For $i=1,2,\cdots,d$, let $N_i$ be the number of vertices at odd levels that are both in the open cluster containing the root and $T_{r_i}$, where we recall $T_{r_i}$ denotes the subtree rooted at $r_i$.
Let $B_i=\one{[r_i \text{ is open}]}\ (i=1,\cdots,d).$
Then,
\begin{align*}
    \E{N \wedge n} 
    &= \E{\tp{N_e+\sum_{i=1}^d N_i} \wedge n}\\
    & \leq \E{N_e\wedge n+\sum_{i=1}^d \tp{N_i\wedge n}}\\
    &= \E{N_e\wedge n}+\sum_{i=1}^d \Pr{B_i=1}\E{N_i\wedge n\vert B_i=1}.
\end{align*}
By \cref{lem:percolation-even}, $\E{N_e\wedge n}=O_{c_1}(\sqrt{n})$.
Similarly, $\E{N_i\wedge n\vert B_i=1}=O_{c_1}(\sqrt{n})$ for any $1\leq i\leq d$.

Therefore, there exists a constant $K=K(c_1)$ depending only on $c_1$, such that 
\begin{align*}
    \E{N \wedge n} 
    &\leq \E{N_e\wedge n}+\sum_{i=1}^d \Pr{B_i=1}\E{N_i\wedge n\vert B_i=1}\\
    &\leq K\sqrt{n}\tp{1+\sum_{i=1}^d \Pr{B_i=1}}\\
    &\leq K\sqrt{n}(1+c_2). \tag{\cref{eq:root_children_prob}}
\end{align*}
This shows $\E{N \wedge n} = O_{c_1,c_2}(\sqrt{n})$ as desired.
\end{proof}

We next prove \cref{lem:percolation-even} by considering the process of site percolation described in \cref{subsec:Hardcore-game}.


\begin{proof}[Proof of \cref{lem:percolation-even}]

Recall that in \cref{subsec:Hardcore-game}, we introduced a process of site percolation working in rounds that reveals the status of all children and grandchildren of an active vertex in each round.
We note that the process in \cref{subsec:Hardcore-game} is described under an adversary setting.
Here, we restate this process more precisely for a fixed site percolation model.
For convenience, we call it the \emph{site percolation process}.

Let $A_t$ be the number of active vertices after round $t$.
Let $U_t$ be the set of active vertices after round $t$.
We label all the vertices in $V$ by $1,2,\cdots$.
At first, only the root is open and active, and the status of all other vertices is unrevealed.
Therefore, $A_0=1$, $U_0=\set r$.
For any $t\in \Z^+$, at the beginning of round $t$, assuming $A_{t-1}\geq1$, we choose the active vertex with the least label in the current set of active vertices $U_{t-1}$, denoted by $v_t$.
Then reveal the status of all the children and grandchildren of $v_t$. 
To be specific, for each child or grandchild of $v_t$, denoted by $u$, sample $B_u\sim\-{Ber}(p_u)$ independently, then let $u$ be open if $B_u=1$; be closed if $B_u=0$.
Let $Q_t$ be the set of activated grandchildren of $v_t$. 
We note that if a grandchild is activated, then it is open; however, the converse does not hold.
Let $X_t$ be the number of activated grandchildren of $v_t$, i.e., $X_t=|Q_t|$.
Then $U_t=U_{t-1}\setminus\set{v_t}\cup Q_t$, and $A_t=A_{t-1}-1+X_t$.
The process stops when there are no active vertices, i.e., the first time $A_t=0$.
If the process stops after $k$ rounds, we have $N_e=k$, because every vertex in the open cluster containing the root at even levels becomes active at some time and will be deactivated later in the process, and the number of rounds equals the number of deactivated vertices.
If the process never stops, we have $N_e=\infty$.

Let $\VV$ be the set of all possible active vertex sets of the site percolation process.
For any $m,a\in \Z_{\geq0}, S\in \VV$ with $a = |S|$, define the \emph{winning probability} $\psi_m(a,S)$ of the size percolation process by the probability that the site percolation process will last for at least $m$ more rounds when currently there are $a$ active vertices and the set of active vertices is $S$.
Then, for any $m\in \Z^+$, we have $\Pr{N_e\geq m}=\psi_m(1,\set{r})$.

Let $\pi_t$ be the law of $X_t$. We next find a family of distributions that contains all possible $\pi_t$.
Let $u_1,\cdots, u_d$ be $d$ children of $v_t$.
Let $Z_i$ be the number of children of $u_i$ that are activated in round $t$.
Then, we have
\begin{align*}
    X_t=\sum_{i=1}^d Z_i 
    \quad \-{and} \quad
    \E{Z_i}=p_{u_i}\sum_{w\in L(u_i)}p_w\leq\frac{1}{d}\tp{1+\frac{c_1}{\sqrt n}}.
\end{align*}
Then, $\pi_t=\zeta_1 * \zeta_2 * \cdots *\zeta_d$, where $\zeta_i$ is the law of $Z_i$.    
Let $\DD$ be a family of distributions defined by 
\begin{align*}
    \DD=\set{\mu_1*\cdots*\mu_d \,\bigg\vert\, \mathbb{E}_{\mu_i}[X]\leq \frac 1d \tp{1+\frac{c_1}{\sqrt n}},i=1\cdots,d},
\end{align*}
where $\mu_1,\cdots,\mu_d$ are distributions on $\Z_{\geq0}$.
Then for any $t\in \Z^+$, $\pi_t\in\DD$, i.e., $\DD$ is a family of distributions that contains all possible $\pi_t$.

The site percolation process defined above can be considered as a strategy of the online decision-making game introduced in \cref{sec:game} with $\PP=\DD$. (We note that this strategy is slightly different from the strategy defined in \cref{subsec:game-setup}, which we will discuss later.)
This inspires us to consider the online decision-making game with $\PP=\DD$.

We next check $\DD$ satisfies the conditions in \cref{thm:game-main}.
It is easy to check that the metric space $(\DD, d_{\mathrm{TV}})$ is compact. 
Let $\gamma=\frac{1}{d}\tp{1+\frac{c_1}{\sqrt n}}$.
We next show $\-{Bin}(d,\gamma)$ is the largest distribution in $\DD$ under ``$\oleq$''.
First of all, $\-{Bin}(d,\gamma)= \tp{\-{Ber}(\gamma)}^{*d} \in\DD$, where we recall that for a distribution $\mu$, $\mu^{*t}$ is the $t$-fold convolution of $\mu$ with itself (see \cref{def:t-fold-conv}).
For any $\mu\in \DD$, we may assume $\mu=\mu_1*\cdots*\mu_d$, where $\mu_1,\cdots,\mu_d$ are distributions on $\Z_{\geq0}$ with expectations at most $\gamma$.
For any $1\leq i \leq d$, since $\E[\mu_i]{X}\leq \gamma$, by \cref{lem:sd-ber}, we have $\mu_i\oleq \-{Ber}\tp{\gamma}$. 
By \cref{lem:sd-conv}, we have $\mu=\mu_1*\cdots*\mu_d\oleq \-{Ber}\tp{\gamma}*\cdots*\-{Ber}\tp{\gamma}=\tp{\-{Ber}\tp{\gamma}}^{*d} =\-{Bin}\tp{d,\gamma}$.
Therefore, $\-{Bin}(d,\gamma)$ is the largest distribution in $\DD$ under ``$\oleq$''.
Furthermore, it is clear that $\-{Bin}(d,\gamma)$ is a Poisson binomial distribution with expectation $d\gamma=1+\frac{c_1}{\sqrt n}\geq 1$.

Therefore, by \cref{thm:game-main}, for the online decision-making game with $\PP=\DD$, there is a optimal strategy $\SS^*(m,a)\equiv \pi^*:=\-{Bin}(d,\gamma)$, and the player can achieve the maximum wining probability by using this strategy, i.e., by choosing $\pi_t=\pi^*:=\-{Bin}(d,\gamma)$ for each round $t\in\Z^+$.

As we mentioned above, the site percolation process can be considered as a strategy for a player playing the online decision-making game with $\PP=\DD$. 
However, the strategy of the player here is distinct from the strategies defined in \cref{subsec:game-setup}, in the sense that the player (the site percolation process) maintains extra storage and uses external randomness (i.e., the evolution of the set of active vertices) beyond provided in the game (the target number of rounds to survive and the number of tokens) to make a decision in each round.
However, it is not hard to see intuitively that an optimal strategy should not require any other information and should depend only on $m$, the target number of rounds to survive, and $a$, the current number of tokens. Namely, the strategy from the site percolation process is no better than an optimal oblivious strategy as defined in \cref{subsec:game-setup}. We formally state this in the following claim, whose proof is similar to \cref{lem:game_recurance}.

\begin{claim}\label{claim:percolation-strategy}
    For any $m\in \Z^+, a\in\Z_{\geq0},S\in\VV$ with $a = |S|$, it holds that
    \begin{align*}
        \psi_m(a,S) \leq \varphi^{*}_m(a),  
    \end{align*}
    where $\varphi^{*}_m(a)$ is the maximum winning probability of the online decision-making game with $\PP=\DD$ when the player needs to survive $m$ more rounds to win and the current number of tokens is $a$.
\end{claim}

\cref{claim:percolation-strategy} implies $\Pr{N_e \geq m}=\psi_m(1,\set r)\leq \varphi^*_m(1)$ holds for any $m\in \Z^+$.
We next bound $\varphi^*_m(1)$.
By \cref{item:game-main-2} of \cref{thm:game-main}, we have for any $m,a \in \Z^+$,
    \begin{align*}
        \varphi^*_m(a) =\Pr{\tau_{-a}\geq m},
    \end{align*}
where $\tau_{-a}$ is defined in the same way as in \cref{item:game-main-2} of \cref{thm:game-main}. To be specific, let $Z_1,Z_2,\cdots$ be independent and identically distributed random variables with distribution $\pi^*=\-{Bin}(d,\gamma)$,
and let $S_t=\sum_{i=1}^t (Z_i-1)$, then $\tau_{-a}$ is defined by $\tau_{-a}:=\min\{t\geq0 \mid S_t=-a\}$, with the convention that $\tau_{-a}:=\infty$ if $S_t \neq -a$ for all $t\geq0$.
Then $\Pr{N_e \geq m}\leq \varphi^*_m(1)=\Pr{\tau_{-1}\geq m}$ holds for any $m\in \Z^+$.

Let $P^*=\set{p_v^*}_{v\in V}$ be an occupation probability list satisfying $p^*_r=1$, and $p^*_v=\gamma$ for all $v\in V\setminus\set r$.
It is straightforward to check that $\tau_{-1} \overset{d}{=} N(\T_{d}^{\-{ary}},P^*)$, where $N(\T_{d}^{\-{ary}},P^*)$ is the random variable representing the size of the open cluster containing the root in the site percolation on $\T_{d}^{\-{ary}}$ with occupation probability $\gamma$ for all non-root vertices.
For simplicity of notation, we write $N^*$ as shorthand for $N(\T_{d}^{\-{ary}},P^*)$.
By \cite[Lemmas 4.7 and 4.8]{CCYZ24}, we have 
    \begin{align*}
        \Pr{N^*= \infty}=O_{c_1}(n^{-\frac{1}{2}})
        \quad \-{and} \quad
        \Pr{N^*= \ell}=O(\ell^{-\frac{3}{2}}).
    \end{align*}
Then, for any $m\in \Z^+$,
\begin{align*}
    \Pr{N_e \geq m}\leq\Pr{\tau_{-1}\geq m} 
    = \Pr{N^*\geq m}
    &= \sum_{\ell=m}^{\infty} \Pr{N^* = \ell} + \Pr{N^*=\infty}\\
    &\leq  K_1 \tp{\sum_{\ell=m}^{\infty} \ell^{-\frac32} + n^{-\frac{1}{2}}}
    \leq  K_2 \tp{m^{-\frac12} + n^{-\frac{1}{2}}},
\end{align*}    
where $K_1=K_1(c_1), K_2=K_2(c_1)$ are constants depending only on $c_1$.
Therefore, 
\begin{align*}
    \E{N_e \wedge n} = \sum_{m=1}^n \Pr{N_e \geq m}\leq\sum_{m=1}^n K_2 \tp{m^{-\frac12} + n^{-\frac{1}{2}}} = O_{c_1}(\sqrt{n}),
\end{align*}
as desired.
\end{proof}
To complete the proof of \cref{lem:percolation-even}, we prove \cref{claim:percolation-strategy} by induction.
\begin{proof}[Proof of \cref{claim:percolation-strategy}]
   We prove by induction on $m$.

   For the base case $m=1$, by definition, it is easy to check
   \begin{align*}
       \psi_1(a,S) = \varphi^*_1(a)=\one[a\geq 1]
   \end{align*}
   holds for any $a\in \Z_{\geq0},S\in\VV$ with $a=|S|$.

   Now suppose $m\geq2$ and \cref{claim:percolation-strategy}
   holds for $m-1$. 
   We aim to prove \cref{claim:percolation-strategy}
   holds for $m$.

   For $a=0$, by definition, $\psi_m(0,\emptyset) = \varphi^*_m(0)=0$.
   We now assume $a\geq 1$.
   For any $S\in\VV$ with $|S|=a$, consider that at the beginning of some round of the site percolation process, the current set of activated vertices is $S$, and the process needs to last for $m$ more rounds to win.
   Let $X$ be the number of vertices activated in this round, and $S'$ be the set of active vertices after this round.
   Let $\hat\pi$ be the law of $X$.
   Then $\hat\pi\in \DD$.
   After this round, the site percolation process needs to last for $m-1$ more rounds to win, and there are $a-1+X$ active vertices, and the set of active vertices is $S'$.
   Let $\LL$ be the law of $(X,S')$.
   Then,
   \begin{align*}
       \psi_m(a,S)
       &= \E[(X,S')\sim \LL]{\psi_{m-1}(a-1+X,S')}\\
       &\leq \E[(X,S')\sim \LL]{\varphi_{m-1}^{*}(a-1+X)}\tag{induction hypothesis} \\
       &=\E[X\sim \hat\pi]{\varphi_{m-1}^{*}(a-1+X)}\\
       &\leq \sup_{\pi\in\DD} \E[X\sim \pi]{\varphi_{m-1}^*(a-1+X)}\tag{$\hat\pi\in\DD$}\\
       &= \varphi_m^*(a) \tag{\cref{lem:game_recurance}}.
   \end{align*}
   Therefore, \cref{claim:percolation-strategy}
   holds for $m$.
   By the induction principle, \cref{claim:percolation-strategy} holds for all $m\in \Z^+$.
\end{proof}

\section{Proofs of Spectral Independence and Rapid Mixing}
\label{sec:proof-SI-mixing}
In this section, we aim to prove spectral independence and rapid mixing of the critical hardcore model.

In \cref{subsec:critical-SI}, we prove spectral independence of the critical hardcore model by \cref{thm:percolation-full}.
In \cref{subsec:critical-rapid-mixing}, we derive rapid mixing of the critical hardcore model by spectral independence.

\subsection{Spectral independence: Proof of \texorpdfstring{\cref{thm:SI-critical}}{Theorem 2.3}}\label{subsec:critical-SI}

To prove \cref{thm:SI-critical}, we first transfer the problem of upper-bounding the spectral independence into a problem of site percolation on trees in the way described in \cref{subsec:CI-to-SI,subsec:sp-to-coupling}.
To this end, \cref{prop:CI-to-SI} and the following proposition are needed, and the latter immediately follows from the construction of the greedy coupling in \cref{subsec:CI-to-SI} and the site percolation in \cref{subsec:sp-to-coupling}.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:SP-to-CI}
    Let $T=(V,E)$ be a tree rooted at $r$.
    Consider the site percolation model on $T$ with occupation probability list
    $P=\{p_v\}_{v \in V}$ where $p_r = 1$ and $p_v= \Pr[\mu_{T_v,\lambda}]{\sigma_v = 1}\ (v\in V\setminus\set r)$.
    Let $N(T,P)$ be the size of the open cluster containing the root.
    Let $\CC$ be the greedy coupling of $(X,Y)$ on $T$ constructed in \cref{subsec:CI-to-SI}, where $X$ is an independent set distributed as $\Pr[\mu_{T,\lambda}]{\;\cdot \mid \sigma_r = 1}$ and $Y$ as $\Pr[\mu_{T,\lambda}]{\;\cdot \mid \sigma_r = 0}$, such that 
    \begin{align*}
        \left| X \oplus Y \right| \overset{d}{=} N(T,P),
    \end{align*}
    where $(X,Y)\sim \CC$, and recall that ``$\ \overset{d}{=}$'' represents that the two random variables are equal in distribution.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof of \cref{thm:SI-critical}]
We first transfer the problem to upper-bounding the size of the open cluster containing the root of a site percolation on the infinite $\Delta$-regular tree.
    Let $T=(V_T,E_T)$ be a tree rooted at $r$ with
maximum degree at most $\Delta$.
For each vertex $v \in V_T$, let $p'_v$ denote the probability that $v$ is occupied in the hardcore model with fugacity $\lambda$ on the subtree $T_v$ rooted at $v$, i.e., $p'_v := \Pr[\mu_{T_v,\lambda}]{\sigma_v = 1}$.
    Since $T$ has maximum degree at most $\Delta$, $T$ can be considered as a subtree of the infinite $\Delta$-regular tree $\T_{\Delta}^{\-{reg}}=(V,E)$, and $V_T \subseteq V$.
    Consider a site percolation model on $\T_{\Delta}^{\-{reg}}$ with occupation probability list $\hat P=\{\hat p_v\}_{v \in V}$ defined by 
    \begin{align*}
        \hat p_v :=\begin{cases}
            1, &v=r\\
            p'_v, &v\in V_T\setminus \set{r}\\
            0, &v\in V \setminus V_T
        \end{cases}.
    \end{align*}
    Let $N=N(\T_{\Delta}^{\-{reg}},\hat P)$ be the size of the open cluster containing the root.
    By \cref{prop:CI-to-SI,prop:SP-to-CI} 
    it suffices to prove
    \begin{align*}
        \E{N\wedge n} = O_{\alpha}(\sqrt{n}).
    \end{align*}
    We next decompose the site percolation model on $\T_{\Delta}^{\-{reg}}$ into two similar site percolation models on $\T_{d}^{\-{ary}}$.
    Let $r_1$ be a child of $r$.
    Let $T_1=T_{r_1}=(V_1,E_1)$ be the subtree of $\T_{\Delta}^{\-{reg}}$ rooted at $r_1$ consisting all descendants of $r_1$.
    Let $T_2=\T_{\Delta}^{\-{reg}}[V\setminus V_1]$ be the subtree induced in $\T_{\Delta}^{\-{reg}}$ by $V\setminus V_1$.
    It is obvious that $T_1$ and $T_2$ are both infinite $d$-ary trees, where $d=\Delta-1$.
    Let $N_i$ be the number of vertices both in $T_i$ and the open cluster containing $r$ $(i=1,2)$.
    Let $B_1=\one[r_1 \-{\ is\ open }]$.
    Then
    \begin{align*}
        \E{N\wedge n}
        &= \E{(N_1+N_2)\wedge n} 
        \leq \E{N_1\wedge n} + \E{N_2\wedge n}\\
        &= \E{N_1\wedge n \vert B_1=1}\Pr{B_1=1} + \E{N_2\wedge n}\\
        &\leq \E{N_1\wedge n \vert B_1=1} + \E{N_2\wedge n}.
    \end{align*}
    It suffices to prove 
    \begin{align*}
        \E{N_1\wedge n \vert B_1=1}=O_{\alpha}(\sqrt n) 
        \quad \-{and}\quad
        \E{N_2\wedge n}=O_{\alpha}(\sqrt n).
    \end{align*}
    We next show $\E{N_1\wedge n \vert B_1=1}=O_{\alpha}(\sqrt n)$.
    The proof for $\E{N_2\wedge n}=O_{\alpha}(\sqrt n)$ is the same.
    We now assume $B_1=1$, i.e., $r_1$ is open.
    Consider the site percolation on $T_1=(V_1,E_1)$ with occupation probability list $P=\set{p_v}_{v\in V_1}$ defined by
    \begin{align*}
        p_v:=\begin{cases}
            1, &v=r_1\\
            \hat p_v, &v\in V_1\setminus \set{r_1}
        \end{cases}
        =\begin{cases}
            1, &v=r_1\\
            p'_v, &v\in V_T \cap V_1\setminus \set{r_1}\\
            0, &\-{otherwise}
        \end{cases}.
    \end{align*}
    Then $N_1=N(T_1,P)$ when $r_1$ is open. 
    In order to apply \cref{thm:percolation-full}, we next show the site percolation on $T_1$ satisfies \cref{item:thm:percolation-full-1,item:thm:percolation-full-2} of \cref{thm:percolation-full}.
    In the following part, let $L(v)$ denote the set of children of $v$ in $\T_{\Delta}^{\-{reg}}$, let $L_T(v)$ denote the set of children of $v$ in $T$.
    For any $v\in V\setminus\set{r_1}$, if $v\notin T$, then 
    \begin{align*}
        p_v\sum_{w\in L(v)} p_w=0;
    \end{align*}
    if $v\in T$, then by \cref{lem:contract-full}, it holds that
    \begin{align*}
        p_v\sum_{w\in L(v)} p_w=p'_v\sum_{w\in L_T(v)} p'_w\leq \frac{1}{d}\tp{1+\frac{\e\alpha}{\sqrt n}}.
    \end{align*}
    Therefore, \cref{item:thm:percolation-full-1} of \cref{thm:percolation-full} holds for $c_1=\e\alpha$.
    Furthermore,
    \begin{align*}
        \sum_{w\in L(r_1)} p_w = \sum_{w\in L_T(r_1)} p'_w \leq \sum_{w\in L_T(r_1)} \frac{\lambda}{\lambda+1} 
        \leq d\lambda\leq \tp{1+\frac{\alpha}{\sqrt n}} d\lambda_c(\Delta)\leq 8(1+\alpha),
    \end{align*}
    where the first inequality follows from $p'_v\leq\frac{\lambda}{\lambda+1}$ for all $v\in V_T$, which can be derived from \cref{fact:tree-recursion}, and the last inequality follows from $d\lambda_c(\Delta)\leq8$ for any $\Delta\geq 3$.
    Therefore, \cref{item:thm:percolation-full-2} of \cref{thm:percolation-full} holds for $c_2=8(1+\alpha)$.
    Then, by \cref{thm:percolation-full}, 
    \begin{align*}
        \E{N_1\wedge n \vert B_1=1}=\E{N(T_1,P)\wedge n }=O_{\alpha}(\sqrt n),
    \end{align*}
    as desired.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Rapid mixing: Proof of \texorpdfstring{\cref{thm:main}}{Theorem 1.1}}
\label{subsec:critical-rapid-mixing}
In this subsection, we aim to provide a minimal introduction and apply the main spectral independence technique as a black box.
We refer the readers to \cite{Liu23,CCYZ24,CE22} for more background and details.

We first define $\ell_{\infty}$ spectral independence for the hardcore model, and we refer the readers to \cite{FGYZ22} for more background.

\begin{definition}[$\ell_{\infty}$ Spectral independence for hardcore model] \label{def:SI}
    Let $\mu_{G,\lambda}$ be the hardcore Model on a graph $G=(V,E)$ with fugacity $\lambda>0$.
    For any $\eta \geq 1$,
    we say $\mu_{G,\lambda}$ satisfies $\ell_{\infty}$ spectral independence with constant $\eta$ if
    \begin{align*}
        \sum_{v \in V} \left| \Psi_{\mu_{G,\lambda}}(u,v) \right|
        \le \eta
    \end{align*}
    holds for all $u\in V$.
    Furthermore, we say $\mu_{G,\lambda}$ satisfies $\ell_{\infty}$ spectral independence with constant $\eta$ for all pinning if for any two disjointing subsets $S_0,S_1\in V$, where $S_1$ induces no edges, the conditional Gibbs distribution $\nu:=\mu_{G,\lambda}(\cdot \mid \sigma_{S_0} = \*0_{S_0}, \sigma_{S_1} = \*1_{S_1})$ satisfies $\ell_{\infty}$ spectral independence with constant $\eta$, i.e., for any vertex $u\in V\setminus(S_0 \cup S_1 \cup \partial S_1)$
    \begin{align*}
        \sum_{v \in V \setminus (S_0 \cup S_1 \cup \partial S_1)} \left| \Psi_{\nu}(u,v) \right| \le \eta,
    \end{align*}
    where we recall that $\partial S_1$ denotes the set of neighbors of $S_1$ in $G$.
\end{definition}

The following proposition shows that rapid mixing of the critical hardcore model can be derived from spectral independence both in the subcritical regime and at criticality.

\begin{proposition}[Spectral independence implies rapid mixing, \cite{CCYZ24,CE22}]
\label{prop:SS-mixing-full}
    Let $\alpha \ge 0$ be a constant. 
    Consider the hardcore model on an $n$-vertex graph $G=(V,E)$ of maximum degree $\Delta \ge 3$. 
    Suppose the following conditions hold:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item \label{item:SS-mixing-full-1}
        \textbf{Spectral Independence in Subcritical Regime:} For any $\delta \in (0,1)$, $\mu_{G,(1-\delta)\lambda_c(\Delta)}$ satisfies $\ell_{\infty}$ spectral independence with constant $\frac{c^*}{\delta}$ for all pinning, where $c^* > 0$ is an absolute constant;

        \item \label{item:SS-mixing-full-2}
        \textbf{Spectral Independence at Criticality:} For any $\lambda \le (1+\frac{\alpha}{\sqrt{n}}) \lambda_c(\Delta)$, $\mu_{G,\lambda}$ satisfies $\ell_{\infty}$ spectral independence with constant $C_0\sqrt n$ for all pinning, where $C_0 = C_0(\alpha) > 0$ is a constant depending only on $\alpha$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Then, for any $\lambda \le (1+\frac{\alpha}{\sqrt{n}}) \lambda_c(\Delta)$, the mixing time of Glauber dynamics satisfies
    \begin{align*}
        T_{\mathrm{mix}}(P_{\mathrm{GD}}) = O_\alpha\left( n^{2+\frac{c^*}{2}} \log \Delta \right).
    \end{align*}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof of \cref{thm:main}]
    By \cite[Lemma 3.18]{CCYZ24}, \cref{item:SS-mixing-full-1} of \cref{prop:SS-mixing-full} holds with constant $c^*=4\e\tp{1+\frac{1}{\Delta-2}}$.
    For any two disjointing subsets $S_0,S_1\in V$, where $S_1$ induces no edges, the conditional Gibbs distribution $\mu_{G,\lambda}(\cdot \mid \sigma_{S_0} = \*0_{S_0}, \sigma_{S_1} = \*1_{S_1})$ corresponds to the hardcore model on the induced subgraph $G[V\setminus (S_0\cup S_1\cup\partial S_1)]$, which satisfies $\ell_{\infty}$ spectral independence with constant $C_0\sqrt{n}$ according to \cref{thm:SI-critical}, where $C_0=C_0(\alpha) > 0$ is a constant depending only on $\alpha$.
    Therefore, \cref{item:SS-mixing-full-2} of \cref{prop:SS-mixing-full} holds.
    Then, by \cref{prop:SS-mixing-full}, 
    \begin{align*}
        T_{\mathrm{mix}}(P_{\mathrm{GD}}) &= O_\alpha\left( n^{2+\frac{c^*}{2}} \log \Delta \right)
        = O_\alpha\left( n^{2+2\e+\frac{2\e}{\Delta-2}} \log \Delta \right),
    \end{align*}
    as desired.
\end{proof}
The following proposition can be concluded from Lemma 2.3, Lemma 3.13, Theorem 3.16, Lemma 3.17, and Lemma 3.19 of \cite{CCYZ24}.
\begin{proposition}[\cite{CCYZ24,CE22}]\label{prop:SI-to-AT}
    Let $\alpha \ge 0$ be a constant.
    Let $G$ be a $n$-vertex graph with maximum degree $\Delta\geq3$.
    Let $\mu_{G,\lambda}$ be the Gibbs distribution of the hardcore model on $G$ with fugacity $\lambda \le (1+\frac{\alpha}{\sqrt{n}}) \lambda_c(\Delta)$.
    Let $K:[0,1]\to \R_{\ge 0}$ be a function such that for every $\eta \in [0,1]$, the Gibbs distribution $\mu_{G,(1-\eta)\lambda}$
    satisfies $\ell_{\infty}$ spectral independence with constant $K(\eta)$ for all pinning.
    For any $\theta\in[0,1]$, if $(1-\theta)\lambda\leq\frac{1}{2\Delta}$, then the mixing time of Glauber dynamics satisfies
    \begin{align*}
        T_{\mathrm{mix}}(P_{\mathrm{GD}}) \leq 
        Cn^2\exp\tp{\int_0^{\theta}\frac{K(\eta)}{1-\eta}\-d\eta}\log\Delta,
    \end{align*}
    where $C=C(\alpha)>0$ is a constant depending only on $\alpha$. 
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof of \cref{prop:SS-mixing-full}]
    Fix $\alpha \geq 0$. 
    By conditions \cref{item:SS-mixing-full-1} and \cref{item:SS-mixing-full-2} of \cref{prop:SS-mixing-full}, for every $\eta \in [0,1]$, $\mu_{G,(1-\eta)\lambda}$ satisfies $\ell_{\infty}$ spectral independence with constant $K(\eta)$ for all pinning, where
    \begin{align*}
        K(\eta)=
        \begin{cases}
            C_0\sqrt{n}, &0\leq \eta \leq \frac{\alpha}{\alpha+ \sqrt n}\\
            \min\tp{\frac{c^*}{\eta-(1-\eta)\alpha/ \sqrt n},C_0\sqrt{n}}, &  \frac{\alpha}{\alpha+ \sqrt n} < \eta \leq 1
        \end{cases}.
    \end{align*}
    In order to apply \cref{prop:SI-to-AT}, we next bound $\exp\tp{\int_0^{\theta}\frac{K(\eta)}{1-\eta}\-d\eta}$.

    \noindent When $\frac{c^*+\alpha}{\sqrt n}\leq \theta$, i.e., $n \geq \tp{\frac{c^*+\alpha}{\theta}}^2$, it holds that 
    \begin{align}\label{eq:expSI}
        \exp\tp{\int_0^{\theta}\frac{K(\eta)}{1-\eta}\-d\eta}
        \leq \exp\tp{C_0\sqrt{n}\int_0^{\frac{c^*+\alpha}{\sqrt n}}\frac{1}{1-\eta}\-d\eta
        +\int_{\frac{c^*+\alpha}{\sqrt n}}^{\theta}\frac{c^*}{\eta-(1-\eta)\alpha/ \sqrt n}\frac{1}{1-\eta}\-d\eta}.
    \end{align}
    For the first term of the exponent of the RHS of \cref{eq:expSI}, when $\frac{c^*+\alpha}{\sqrt n}\leq \frac{1}{2}$, it holds that
    \begin{align}\label{eq:expSI-first-term}
        C_0\sqrt{n}\int_0^{\frac{c^*+\alpha}{\sqrt n}}\frac{1}{1-\eta} \-d \eta \leq 2 C_0\sqrt{n} \frac{c^*+\alpha}{\sqrt n}= 2 C_0(c^*+\alpha).
    \end{align}
    For the second term of the exponent of the RHS of \cref{eq:expSI}, when $\frac{c^*+\alpha}{\sqrt n}\leq \theta$, it holds that
    \begin{align}\label{eq:expSI-second-term}
        \int_{\frac{c^*+\alpha}{\sqrt n}}^{\theta}\frac{c^*}{\eta-(1-\eta)\alpha/ \sqrt n}\frac{1}{1-\eta}\-d\eta
        &= c^* \int_{\frac{c^*+\alpha}{\sqrt n}}^{\theta}\tp{\frac{1}{\eta-\frac{\alpha/ \sqrt n}{1+\alpha/ \sqrt n}}+\frac{1}{1-\eta}}\-d\eta\nonumber\\
        &\leq c^*\int_{\frac{c^*+\alpha}{\sqrt n}}^{\theta}\tp{\frac{1}{\eta-\alpha/ \sqrt n}+\frac{1}{1-\eta}}\-d\eta\nonumber\\
        &=c^*\tp{\log\tp{\frac{\sqrt n-c^*-\alpha}{c^*}}+\log\tp{\frac{\theta-\alpha/ \sqrt n}{1-\theta}}}\nonumber\\
        & \leq c^*\tp{\log\tp{\frac{\sqrt n}{c^*}}+\log\tp{\frac{\theta}{1-\theta}}}.
    \end{align}
    Let $\theta=\frac{99}{100}$, combining \cref{eq:expSI}, \cref{eq:expSI-first-term} and \cref{eq:expSI-second-term}, it holds that 

    \begin{align*}
        \exp\tp{\int_0^{\theta}\frac{K(\eta)}{1-\eta}\-d\eta}
        &\leq \exp \tp{2 C_0(c^*+\alpha)+ c^*\tp{\log\tp{\frac{\sqrt n}{c^*}}+\log\tp{\frac{\theta}{1-\theta}}}}\\
        &=99\exp\tp{2 C_0(c^*+\alpha)-c^*\log c^*}n^{\frac{c^*}{2}}=O_\alpha(n^{\frac{c^*}{2}}),
    \end{align*}
when $n \geq \max\set{\tp{\frac{c^*+\alpha}{\theta}}^2,\tp{2(c^*+\alpha)}^2}=4(c^*+\alpha)^2$.

    When $n\geq\alpha^2$, $(1-\theta)\lambda \leq(1-\theta)(1+\frac{\alpha}{\sqrt n})\lambda_c(\Delta)\leq\frac{1}{100}(1+1)\frac{12}{\Delta}\leq\frac{1}{2\Delta}$, where the second inequality follows from $n\geq\alpha^2$ and $\lambda_c(\Delta)\leq\frac{12}{\Delta}$ for all $\Delta\geq 3$. 
    Then, for $n$ sufficiently large, specifically $n\geq \max\set{4(c^*+\alpha)^2,\alpha^2}=4(c^*+\alpha)^2$, by \cref{prop:SI-to-AT}, we have
    \begin{align*}
        T_{\mathrm{mix}}(P_{\mathrm{GD}}) &\leq 
        Cn^2\exp\tp{\int_0^{\theta}\frac{K(\eta)}{1-\eta}\-d\eta}\log\Delta=O_\alpha\tp{n^{2+\frac{c^*}{2}}\log\Delta},
    \end{align*}
    where $C=C(\alpha)>0$ is a constant depending only on $\alpha$.
\end{proof}



